\par\par \b \uc2 Treasurys Fall On Debt Supply, Reduced Bets On Fed Bond Buying\b0\par\par\uc2 By Min Zeng   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 788 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 20:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Treasurys fell Thursday, hurt by selling related to new corporate and government agency debt supply and as the latest round of U.S. data eased speculation the Federal Reserve may conduct larger-scale purchases in Treasurys.\par \par \uc2 The 10-year and 30-year Treasurys led the selling. The 30-year bond yield touched 3.959%, the highest level since Aug. 13.\par \par \uc2 These long-dated maturities extend losses made Wednesday, as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in six years spurred many investors to shift money into short-dated notes that are believed to be Japan's favored places to park its dollar proceeds.\par \par \uc2 "There are so many cross currents," and Treasurys are "the whipping post," said Thomas Roth at Mitsubishi UFJ Securities (USA).\par \par \uc2 As of 2:24 p.m. EDT, the benchmark 10-year Treasury note was down 6/32 in price and its yield rose to 2.744%. The 30-year bond was down 16/32 to yield 3.905%. Bond yields move inversely to prices.\par \par \uc2 Short-dated Treasurys outperformed, with the two-year note trading flat to yield 0.48% and the three-year note flat to yield 0.763%.\par \par \uc2 Supply of new corporate and agency debt has been brisk this month as sellers take advantage of low interest rates to refinance debt. They typically sell Treasurys to neutralize the risks of higher interest rates, making the debt sales more cost-effective. Once the debt deals are priced, companies and government agencies will unwind the hedges by buying back Treasurys, adding to volatile trading in the bond market.\par \par \uc2 Among the most notable for new debt supply is the \uc2 Tennessee Valley Authority\uc2  (TVA), a federally chartered company. The TVA plans to sell a 50-year bond with a size to be at least $500 million. The deal will be priced later Thursday, which weighed especially on the 30-year Treasury bond.\par \par \uc2 Trading in the bond market has been choppy in recent sessions as a rally has faded. Many data released this month have been less dire than forecast, easing worries about the U.S. economy headed into a double-dip recession in the second half of the year. That cast doubt on whether the Fed plans to carry out larger-scale purchases in Treasurys in coming months to support the economy, and Thursday's data helped ease such speculation again.\par \par \uc2 The latest jobless claims posted an unexpected drop, showing some hope for the labor market, while the business outlook index from the Federal Reserve Bank of Philadelphia was below zero for a second month and worse than forecast. Meanwhile, wholesale prices rose last month but remained tame.\par \par \uc2 The talk about the Fed was "pushed to the back burner today," said Charles Comiskey, head of Treasury trading at Scotia Capital in New York, adding that recent data suggested that it is less likely for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to announce larger-scale Treasury bond buying when policymakers meet Tuesday to set interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark 10-year note's yield has risen from 2.418% hit on Aug. 25, the lowest level since January 2009 and less than 50 basis points away from the record low hit in mid-December 2008.\par \par \uc2 David Coard, head of fixed-income trading in New York at Williams Capital Group, said the bond market is in a "minor correction" of the recent rally. But he said the economy is still vulnerable and the labor market remains weak, adding he is still bullish on Treasurys.\par \par \uc2 Coard said if the 10-year yield rises to between 2.9% and 3%, he will buy again.\par \par \uc2 Japanese investors have been big buyers of Treasurys over the past few months amid concern about the health of the U.S. economy, while a rising yen, which hit a 15-year high Wednesday before a steep drop following \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had made Treasurys cheaper to buy.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is getting closer to reclaiming the No. 1 foreign owner spot of Treasurys that it ceded to China in September 2008. The nation's overall holdings increased by $18 billion in July to $821 billion, far bigger than $3 billion gain from China to $846.7 billion, the latest data from the Treasury Department showed Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "It would not be a total surprise to see Japan as the No. 1 holder of Treasurys by the winter," said Dan Greenhaus, chief economic strategist at Miller Tabak & Co.\par \par \uc2 -By Min Zeng, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2229; min.zeng@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Prabha Natarajan, Tom Barkley and Jeff Bater contributed to this article.) [ 09-16-10 1445ET ]\par \par \uc2 1079 20715 4041   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g000cv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
