\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Geithner Testimony Unlikely To Change  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Course\b0\par\par\uc2 1,281 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 16:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1458 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY holding gains and steady at 85.71, from 85.63 late Wed. While China's policy facing increasing scrutiny from US officials, Geithner testimony unlikely to lead to any immediate real wide swings in USD/JPY or cease \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy, said Steven Barrow, head of G10 strategy at Standard Bank in London. "Stealing competitiveness from your peers can't be well-received from Geithner and others," said Barrow. "That said, it likely won't change anything with the dollar-yen," short-term, because \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has already been taken, and what is done is done, Barrow said. Geithner's comments are unlikely to deter \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , because it has already acted once and will follow through on its own strategy, he said. (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1455 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CAD has been caught in a choppy range trade since the beginning of summer, but the break out could be violent when it finally comes. "At some point, likely late this year or early next, USD/CAD will find the catalyst sufficiently strong enough to push it to a new level," says Scotia Capital. "Historically, when USD/CAD has broken out of its range, it has been a rapid, violent and sustained break, which catches most by surprise, Scotia adds. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1452 GMT [Dow Jones] Much of the agency debt market participants are on a quest this morning to find out more details about a bond, specifically a 50-year issue from the \uc2 Tennessee Valley Authority\uc2 , which is a GSE. However, information is scarce, and book-runners \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  and \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  have put out little. Talk is that the issue, expected to be of size $500 million, is created for investors who already have put their names down. (prabha.natarajan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1447 GMT [Dow Jones] Polish bonds yields are stable after a strong primary auction of 10-year bonds in Poland on Wednesday and a EUR1 billion eurobond issue on Thursday, with both issues strongly oversubscribed, a trader says. Adds yields are expected lower in the longer term, particularly for longer maturities. Average yield of 2-year bonds is flat from Wednesday afternoon at 4.63%, 5-year flat at 5.10%, 10-year at 5.45% from 5.46%. EUR/PLN is at 3.9467 from 3.9357, USD/PLN at 3.0144 from 3.0258. (marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1445 GMT [Dow Jones] New muni issues this week "were pretty well digested," says \uc2 Eaton Vance\uc2 's Evan Rourke, who adds, "there's cash in the market looking to be put to work." Muni prices are holding steady Thursday, despite choppiness in Treasurys.(romy.varghese@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1444 GMT [Dow Jones] Brokers report numerous buyers of a March 2011 put options spread in Eurodollar futures, envisioning lower prices and expectations for higher yields in 1Q next year. Traders performed 30K to 50K put spreads with maximum profit achieved if Mar '11 price falls to 99.25 or lower and implied 3-month dollar Libor climbs to 0.75% or higher. Mar recently trading 0.5 BP higher at 99.54, projecting 0.46% Libor at Mar. 14, 2011 contract expiration. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1436 GMT [Dow Jones] With interest rates low, companies are leaning heavily on the corporate bond market to meet their funding needs, leading to a shrinking commercial paper market. This week through Wed, the commercial paper market fell by $22.9B on a seasonally adjusted basis and $5.6B on an adjusted basis. Overall, the market is now at $1.036 trillion, less than half of the peak of $2.2 trillion in July 2007. (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1437 GMT [Dow Jones] The Turkish lira is slightly weaker on profit-taking after recent gains. USD/TRY trades at 1.4970, from 1.4910 Wednesday. Meanwhile bonds are flat ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary policy committee's rate setting meeting Thursday evening. The benchmark April 25, 2012 bond ends trade at 8.09%, unchanged from Wednesday but it moves to 8.13% in Friday value-dated transactions. Analysts forecast TRY will trade between 1.4850 and 1.5010 and the benchmark bond between 8.00% and 8.15%, in the short term.\par \par \uc2 1430 GMT [Dow Jones] The ruble's current weakness should be viewed as a buying opportunity "to get in on the global growth trade at a cheap level," \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  says. "If optimism regarding the global economy continues, higher oil prices should support the ruble. The ruble is the worst performing emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far in the third quarter, which we do not think is warranted given the improved global outlook." Additionally, the ruble may be helped by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which recently published a paper calling for "gradually changing" the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's floating corridor to increase exchange rate flexibility, the Brown Brother says. Ruble closes at 35.67 against the dollar-euro basket, its weakest levels since February. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1429 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts fall Thursday after demand at an auction fell for the 2nd day running as low yields deterred buyers. The DMO received a cover ratio of 1.68 at a sale of 10-year gilts while the yield tail also widened to 0.5bp vs a cover ratio of 2.45 and a tail of 0.2bp at the last sale. Safe haven bunds also fell after Spanish bond auctions attracted solid demand, amid signs appetite for low yielding government bonds is waning. "Gilts have had an exceptional run but given inflation above 3% and expectations that it'll remain sticky, 10-year yields around 3.10% do look a bit low," says a strategist. Data out earlier showed UK retail sales unexpectedly fell in August although UK's year ahead CPI expectations rose to 3.4% in Aug vs 3.3% in May, the BOE's quarterly poll showed. December gilts are down 0.78 at 122.20 and the 10-year gilt is down at 112.971 to yield 3.154%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1427 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD hits intraday high, at 1.3115 from 1.3012 late Wed, according to EBS via CQG, as investors continue to turn to common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in wake of week's successful euro-zone government debt auctions. Investors also might see EUR as a safety play, says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 's Alan Ruskin, at least as they wait for US economy to emerge from slow patch and await any further moves by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to temper yen strength after Wed's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1424 GMT [Dow Jones] Order books went subject on Ukraine's two-tranche bond deal. Guidance on the 5-year issue is 6 7/8%, while the 10-year bond is expected to price with a yield of 7 7/8%, said a person familiar with the debt. The offer comes two months after Ukraine delayed a bond sale due to financing made available by the IMF. Bookrunners are \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 , \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  and VTB. The country's bonds are trading up on the grey market, said an investor, in a sign of confidence in the notes. Yet, back in July, several investors were hesitant to buy Ukrainian paper under 8%, given corporates regularly issue above that. The IMf deal is credited for the change. (riva.froymovich@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 1059ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g000b2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
