\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Treasurys Up Before PPI, Jobless Claims\b0\par\par\uc2 1,454 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 14:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1209 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys are up as soft equities bolsters demand for safe assets. Long-dated maturities recover from Wednesday's selling after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's first \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 6 years to weaken the yen. The massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had pushed many investors to shift money into short end of the bond market believed to be Japan's favored places to park dollar proceeds. Up next are jobless claims data and PPI at 8:30 a.m. ET, followed by Phila Fed report at 10 a.m. ET. The Fed will buy Treasurys maturing between March 15, 2012 and February 28, 2013 at 10:30 a.m. EDT. 10-yr note is 5/32 higher to yield 2.704%. The 30-yr bond is 2/32 higher to yield 3.873%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1205 GMT [Dow Jones] Amid an atmosphere of criticism following \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and concern over its precedent, market taking cautious approach as it awaits Treasury's Geithner reaction. Geithner scheduled to testify at 10am ET on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. USD/JPY at 85.65, holding steady vs 85.63 Wed, according to EBS via CQG. EUR/USD up at 1.3085 from 1.3012 from late Wed, while GBP/USD down slightly at 1.5614 from 1.5623. Investors also await new US data set comprising PPI results for Aug and new weekly jobless claims.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1207 GMT [Dow Jones] EUROPEAN SUMMARY: The dollar is generally lower in a stop driven session while equities struggled and European government bonds suffered. All eyes were on USD/JPY following Wednesday's massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the BOJ. However the spot kept to a tight 84.40-70 range in Europe with some chatter that the BOJ was sitting on the bid. Instead all the action came in the euro. EUR/USD's break above its recent 1.3037 high came just after the results of 2 very successful Spanish auctions hit the wires and the spot eventually capped out at a fresh one month high of 1.3110. That move propelled EUR/JPY to a 5 week high of 112.33 and EUR/GBP a 7 week high of 0.8402. The latter was helped by a sharply disappointing UK August retail sales report which came in at -0.5% against expectations of a 0.3 gains. GBP/USD initially slumped to 1.5539 from 1.56 but made back the loss as the dollar slipped elsewhere. Late session the SNB kept its reference rate at 0.25% as widely expected however it sharply cut its inflation forecasts for 2010, 2011 and 2012 and said it did not rule out deflation in 2011. The swiss franc weakened sharply on the SNB's comments with both EUR/CHF and USD/CHF jumping a full cent to 1.3220 and 1.0105 respectively. Equities struggled all session with the main bourses around 0.3% lower. European government bonds faired little better. The strong Spanish auctions where the yields fell below their market equivalents weighed as did a disappointing 1.68x cover for the Gilt 10 year auction. December gilts are down 0.43 at 122.55 while December bunds are 0.56 lower at 129.56. Gold hit a fresh all-time high of $1277.70oz and oil trades just over $75bbl. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1154 GMT [Dow Jones] The Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's activity index, IBC, reinforces "the view of more moderate GDP growth in Q3," and supports the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's assessment that the economy should be growing at a pace more in line with potential, says RBC's Zeina Latif in a research note. "As long as the temporary inflationary pressures from higher food commodity prices and adverse local weather conditions do not contaminate inflation expectations significantly, then the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should be able to keep the Selic rate stable [at 10.75%] for a prolonged period," Latif says. (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1138 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY has spent the session in a tight 85.40-70 range with some chatter that the BOJ is sitting on the bid. Earlier in the day the spot topped out at 85.78 which exactly matched Wednesday's post-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and September high. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1137 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF appears to be attempting to base says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst Karen Jones. She sees near-term risk for a rise towards the double Fibonacci retracement targets of 1.3195 and 1.3210 and a daily close above the latter would give some confidence to the basing idea. Alternatively, Jones says a break below 1.2851 will bring the recent all-time lows of 1.2765 back into focus. EUR/CHF now at 1.3105. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1119 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/GBP runs into a wall of offers above 0.84 and slips quickly to 0.8370. That move gives GBP/USD a lift and it breaks above 1.5600 to 1.5630 after a disappointing August retail sales number had taken it down to the day's low of 1.5539. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1108 GMT [Dow Jones] The weakening ruble indicates "mispricing based on the fundamental parameters of the Russian economy" and the ruble should be higher with oil at $75 to $80 a barrel, Renaissance Capital says. "We think that opening long ruble positions in the current environment would be an extremely attractive opportunity," RenCap's Anton Nikitin says. Ruble slips to 35.21 against the dollar-euro \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket, from 35.12 late Tuesday. Its down at 30.96 against the dollar and 40.33 vs euro. (william.mauldin@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1107 GMT [Dow Jones] While market sentiment seems to have improved over the past couple of weeks, with economic data no longer surprising on the downside, rising sovereign spreads remain a big threat in the eyes of credit investors, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2 . In fact, SovX Western Europe index is pushing higher, now only 15 bps off the last year's wides, while at 105 bps, the iTraxx Europe corporate index is more that 35 bps inside the wides. \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  adds that credit investors remain more concerned about the resurgence of sovereign/financial problems compared to equity investors. Indeed, they reckon stocks have outperformed CDS by around 5bps, according to their model. Suggests a duration-weighted 3s5s flattener in the Europe index as a bearish hedge to take advantage of the wide basis to theoretical in the 3-year contract. (michael.wilson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1106 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds sharply lower as RBI raised key borrowing rates by higher-than-expected 50 bps to 5%, says trader with state-run bank. Benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond at 98.82 after rising to 99.27 vs 99.02 last close. "From hereon, bonds are likely to gain in the near-term because today's rate hikes indicate that RBI is done with monetary tightening for the rest of this fiscal year and inflation too, is showing signs of moderating," trader says. Adds, benchmark 2020 bond yield may trade near 7.90%-8.00% in coming week with buying interest likely at 8.00%. Tips 2020 bond in 98.77-98.90 band for rest of session. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1106 GMT [Dow Jones] The post-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rise in USD/JPY stopped short of the late August peak near 85.92 which also happens to be the 50-day MA notes \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 . because of this the greater downtrend has not suffered much damage says the bank and with yields on 2-year US Treasurys within striking distance of their August/September lows the risk is that USD/JPY will remain under pressure. Trading at 85.65, near-term support comes in at 84.45 and below that, any move would open risk toward 83.70. Topside, only movement above 86.00 would trigger a stronger recovery says the bank. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1104 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CZK trades little changed at 24.608-24.658 from 24.64 late Wednesday but up from 24.57 earlier Thursday. CZK continues to attract investors looking for a safe haven away from the forint amid concerns about a possible rating cut by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Budapest-issued debt, some traders say. Having broken briefly below support at 25.600, EUR/CZK may continue to test such levels in coming days, one trader says. (leos.rousek@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 0809ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g00087\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
