\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: RBC Not Convinced Greek Restructuring Is Avoidable\b0\par\par\uc2 1,339 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 12:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1030 GMT [Dow Jones] RBC senior credit strategist Simon Ballard isn't convinced by Greek PM Papaconstantinou's comments saying debt restructuring is out of the question. "Perhaps it could manage to avoid 'default' as such, but increasingly the market does seem to be looking to Athens for at least an achievable realignment of its debt profile," says Ballard. "The government may have buoyed its financial hopes by deferring payment obligations for now, but economic reform requires a feasible plan for long-term growth." Says these economic and fiscal challenges look set to determine credit market sentiment for some time, noting that higher yields in the peripheral European telecom and utilities spaces - due to peripheral sovereign exposures - have created a number of attractive longer-term investment opportunities. (michael.wilson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1023 GMT [Dow Jones] While USD/CHF holds below 1.0050-70 resistance the trend is resolutely bearish says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . The bank is looking for a retest of the 2009 low at 0.9915 and a break here would open risk toward the 2008 low of 0.9640. Topside the bank says above 1.0070 should trigger a temporary squeeze higher although the medium-term outlook will remain bearish below 1.0270. USD/JPY now at 1.0028. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1012 GMT [Dow Jones] Hong Kong June-August jobless rate falls to 4.2% vs 4.3% in May-July as strong local economy spurred job creation, government says; jobless rate also lower than 4.3% median forecast of 10 economists polled by Dow Jones. "The latest jobless rate is a positive surprise, indicating domestic demand remains strong," Core Pacific-Yamaichi economist Keith Lo says. Adds, jobless rate likely to trend lower in coming months "but it won't be a big fall as the Europe's sovereign debt crisis could weigh on the city's labor market." Tips jobless rate to fall to 4.0% by year-end. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1008 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF nudges up to a fresh one-week high of 1.3125 ahead of the 1200 GMT SNB interest rate decision. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely expected to keep its reference rate unchanged at 0.25%, however, the monetary report could spring some surprises either on the deflation or inflation front.(gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1005 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares end +0.4% at 924.81 with property stocks offering strong support amid growing expectations BOT may hold rates steady at October meeting in order to slow down capital inflows, stem THB's rise. Still, sharp decline in trading volume today suggests buying interest not strong, investors haven't fully restored their confidence, says SCB Securities Senior VP Sukit Udomsirikul. Resistance tipped at 926, support at 915 Friday. Among top actives, Land & Houses (LH.TH) +6.1% at THB6.95, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) off 0.8% at THB91.75, PTT (PTT.TH) +1.1% at THB284, \uc2 Pruksa Real Estate\uc2 (PS.TH) +2.1% at THB24. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0955 GMT [Dow Jones] The ruble may rally after finding support at RUB31.09 to the dollar and RUB35.27 to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar-euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket, a Moscow trader at an international investment bank said. The ruble slump in the last two days was caused by dollar-euro fluctuations that had the effect of moving traders out of the ruble basket after the Japanese dollar-yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , trader says. Current weakness today is just "echoes of yesterday's selloff," trader says. Ruble on Thursday slips to 35.16 against the dollar-euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket at 0914 GMT, from 35.12 late Tuesday. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls to 30.9720 against the dollar, the weakest since early July, and slumps to 40.3135 vs euro. (william.mauldin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0951 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds mixed in thin trade; offshore players were spotted selling medium- to long-term tenors, says local dealers. Expects rangebound trade Friday; there may be some follow through selling but current levels still look attractive. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.77%/2.72% vs 2.78%/2.72% late yesterday, November 2016 quoted at 2.92%/2.87% vs 2.90%/2.80%, June 2019 quoted at 3.00%/2.95% vs 2.98%/2.92%. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0946 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares end down 0.5% at 3,341.632 in large volume on profit-taking; but foreign fund inflows lend support, lifting index from intraday low of 3,318.523, traders say. "Most locals are cashing in their gains, and this will likely continue tomorrow. On the other hand, foreigners are buying a decent amount of shares," trader says; foreigners net buyers today, picking up around IDR828 billion worth of stocks. Index tipped in 3,260-3,370 range Friday. Among decliners: \uc2 Astra International\uc2  (ASII.JK) down 4.3% at IDR55,300; Telkom (TLKM.JK) down 1.1% at IDR9,200; \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) down 1.5% at IDR6,650; and Bumi Resources (BUMI.JK) down 0.6% at IDR1,820. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0928 GMT [Dow Jones] The unexpected 0.5% drop in UK August retail sales deals a significant blow to growth hopes and will likely fuel fears of a double dip recession says Global Insight's Howard Archer. He notes that the fall in retail sales were not only broad based but also occurred despite evidence of competitive pricing on the high street with the year on year deflator at a 9 month low of 1.5%. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0926 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  economist Christian Keller says Hungary's 2011 financing is risky on uncertainties about the government's final fiscal policy decisions and Prime Minister Viktor Orban's yet unrevealed plans. Expects investors to be hesitant to buy into the presented plan due to high volatility. Says the financing plan requires potential investors to be very confident about Hungary's outlook to be willing to buy into the increased issuance, and adds the government's communication on its strong liquidity position and the feasibility of the financing plan is overly optimistic. (veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0925 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB unchanged vs late yesterday at 30.85. Expectations BOT will keep policy rate steady next month supporting pair but THB widely expected to continue rising after BOT's chief said yesterday bank has no immediate plan to take additional steps to stem THB strength. No explicit sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support USD/THB today. "As fears of (possible) \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures subside, I think the dollar/baht would quickly resume its downward trend," says Bangkok-based dealer. Next support at 30.80, resistance pegged at 30.90.(phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0917 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's large JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday "could be a loud warning shot to Beijing, given the (recent) record buying of Japanese government bonds by China," says Ashraf Laidi, chief market strategist at CMC Markets. China in July increased net buying of Japan financial assets, all or nearly of which thought to be in JGBs. Some analysts speculate China's JGB buying could be meant in part to keep JPY strong, weighing on Japan economy, which China looks to surpass as world's second biggest this year. Japan Finance Minister Noda recently questioned "true intention" of China's JGB purchases. Given Noda raising such suspicions, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  statements on resolve to pursue further action "sound like a warning shot to Beijing's yen-purchases as opposed to the average \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculator," Laidi says. Adds, Beijing could counter by tightening monetary policy, which could upset risk trade, possibly push safe-haven JPY higher.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 0630ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g0007h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
