\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/THB Steady Late But Biased Down; 30.8 Support\b0\par\par\uc2 1,227 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 11:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0925 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB unchanged vs late yesterday at 30.85. Expectations BOT will keep policy rate steady next month supporting pair but THB widely expected to continue rising after BOT's chief said yesterday bank has no immediate plan to take additional steps to stem THB strength. No explicit sign of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support USD/THB today. "As fears of (possible) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  measures subside, I think the dollar/baht would quickly resume its downward trend," says Bangkok-based dealer. Next support at 30.80, resistance pegged at 30.90.(phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0917 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's large JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday "could be a loud warning shot to Beijing, given the (recent) record buying of Japanese government bonds by China," says Ashraf Laidi, chief market strategist at CMC Markets. China in July increased net buying of Japan financial assets, all or nearly of which thought to be in JGBs. Some analysts speculate China's JGB buying could be meant in part to keep JPY strong, weighing on Japan economy, which China looks to surpass as world's second biggest this year. Japan Finance Minister Noda recently questioned "true intention" of China's JGB purchases. Given Noda raising such suspicions, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  statements on resolve to pursue further action "sound like a warning shot to Beijing's yen-purchases as opposed to the average \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculator," Laidi says. Adds, Beijing could counter by tightening monetary policy, which could upset risk trade, possibly push safe-haven JPY higher.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0912 GMT [Dow Jones] Pakistan shares lower as institutions sell, with cement, fertilizers shares leading losses. KSE-100 index last down 0.3% at 10,014.25. "Funds are booking profits at every high levels due to uncertainty and inflation concerns. Markets rose over 1% in early session on approval of margin trading system by SECP and IMF's $450 million aid for the country for emergency needs," says dealer. Mohammad Sohail, analyst at the Topline Securities says investors wary that higher inflation could lead to higher interest rates. DGK Cement (DGKC.KA) down 2.1% at PKR25.38, MCB Bank (MCBB.KA) down 0.2% at PKR193.00. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0905 GMT [Dow Jones] The successful Spanish bond auction Thursday is the most likely trigger for the healthy bounce we have seen in EUR, but it's not a wholly satisfactory explanation. After all, EUR tends to fall on bad auctions but do nothing on good results. That's getting the rumor mill going again. "You can never prove it, but maybe this is some Chinese buying," says Lutz Karpowitz at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . He points out the USD/CNY fixing has fallen a lot by its standards of late, and China has an interest in a weak dollar to keep trade-weighted values favorable. EUR/USD stands at 1.3059. (katie.martin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0904 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PKR tad lower at 85.78 vs 85.79 Wednesday as export settlements, remittances offset demand from imports, oil payments. Still, pair unlikely to fall sharply as demand from importers strong in afternoon session. Almost $27 million-$28 million being bought by oil importers, $10 million-$12 million for payments of non-oil imports, says trader. Tips pair to trade in 85.75-85.85 band. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0903 GMT [Dow Jones] A "modest" $1.6 billion drop in Russia's international reserves in the past week may indicate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  slightly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, selling dollars on behalf of the sinking ruble, \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  says. But most of the decline in reserves in dollar terms probably came from dollar-euro fluctuations, RBS says. Ruble on Thursday slips to 35.15 against the dollar-euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket at 0857 GMT, from 35.12 late Tuesday. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls to 30.9816 against the dollar, the weakest since early July, and slumps to 40.3052 vs euro. (william.mauldin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0901 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP higher late, in line with broad USD strength vs regional units; players continue to cover short positions after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (to weaken JPY vs USD) yesterday. Pair last at 44.40 in heavy volume vs 44.26 late Wednesday. "There are also concerns about the U.S. economy so some investors are shifting funds," says local bank trader. Tips pair may head towards 44.70 resistance Friday; psychological support at 44.00. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0858 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD tad higher at 31.800 late vs 31.798 yesterday, after hovering in 31.74-31.78 band for most of session. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of buying late, may have bought around US$20 million-US$30 million to curb TWD strength, traders say. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been very strict about the Taiwan dollar and I don't think the Taiwan dollar will deviate much from NT$32.00 in the near future," trader says; tips pair in 31.70-31.80 band tomorrow. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0856 GMT [Dow Jones] The Spanish Treasury "might be quite happy with a strong result and EUR4 billion sold," said Jean Francois Robin, strategist at Natixis in Paris, after the Treasury sold the maximum intended EUR4 billion of October 2020 and July 2041-dated government bonds at auction Thursday. "A good auction confirming the recent better mood of the market towards Spain," he says, adding Spain is clearly a bit "off the hook" recently, offering better resistance than Portugal or Ireland in the sell-off and still benefiting from [spread] tightening thanks to better liquidity. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0854 GMT [Dow Jones] The 10-year Spanish/German yield spread remains relatively stable Thursday after the successful auction of the Spanish October 2020 and July 2041 auctions. No real concession had been built into the 10-year bond ahead of the auction, however, Peter Chatwell, Credit Agricole strategist, says the bond was already at cheap levels both against German bunds and Italian BTPs. This week has seen a developing tightening trend between core and peripheral bonds, with bunds moving lower and this helped to restrict the concession building, he says. The 10-year Spanish/German yield spread is 2 bps tighter at 173 bps. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0854 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds extend losses, weighed by sharper-than-expected reverse repo rate hike at RBI policy meet, says trader with primary dealership; 7.80% 2020 bond at 98.75 vs 98.81 earlier, 99.02 last close. Bonds may remain under pressure, recovery unlikely until benchmark bond yield tests 8.00%, says trader. RBI raises reverse repurchase rate by 50 bps, repo rate by 25 bps, surprising market which had mostly expected 25bp hike in both rates.(bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0852 GMT [Dow Jones] Healthy demand at the Spanish 10 and 30 year bond auctions, with sharply lower yields, has helped the euro Thursday. EUR/USD trades at a one month high of 1.3070, from around 1.3025 shortly before the auction results. (gary.stride@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 0525ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g0006x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
