\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Geithner Likely To Avoid Criticizing Japan\b0\par\par\uc2 1,242 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 09:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0742 GMT [Dow Jones] The US House of Representatives Committee of Ways and Means Chairman Levin has had his say describing JPY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as "deeply disturbing." Now it is the turn of Treasury Sec Geithner when he appears before Levin's committee later in the day. "Although Secretary Geithner will likely attempt to avoid outright criticism of Japan, it seems reasonable to suppose that he may repeat the comments he made on TV a week ago, indicating that it would be better served looking for ways to reinforce the recovery," says Bank of New York Mellon. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0741 GMT [Dow Jones] Swedish krona strengthens against the euro, with the EUR/SEK pair touching a near three-year low after stronger-than-expected unemployment data in August. The jobless rate fell to 7.4% from 8% in July against forecasts for 7.8%. EUR/SEK trades at 9.2075 from 9.2206 before the data. (ian.edmondson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0738 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS up after PBOC advisor Li's comments yesterday that raising deposit interest rates would benefit country's financial stability; 1-year +2 bps at 2.13%-2.20%, 5-year +2 bps at 2.83%-2.90%. "There are different opinions in the market. But apparently some traders think a rate hike will come earlier than we original expected," says Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. Li's comments came after he recommended in recent interview on state TV a slight increase in deposit rates to partly offset nearly 2-year-high consumer prices. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0736 GMT [Dow Jones] September HSI futures down 0.4% at 21,699, but same as cash market, futures off intraday low of 21,535. Despite HK market snapping 5-day winning streak, September futures trade at consistent premium to cash throughout session, now around 40 points, vs September fair value about flat, continuing to signal near-tem bullishness. Volume heavier than yesterday at 62,626 lots. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0733 GMT [Dow Jones] India's 10-year benchmark bond yield may face resistance at 8.0% despite bond markets' apparently bearish knee-jerk reaction to RBI's monetary policy, says Ajay Mahajan, treasury head at \uc2 UBS\uc2 . Benchmark bond yield currently at 7.97%. Notes, higher-than-expected reverse repo rate hike may have more to do with realigning discount window corridor than taking more aggressive-than-expected stance on inflation. "Basically, what you should be seeing is a 25 basis point hike in the repo rate," Mahajan notes. While bonds may remain vulnerable to waves of weak sentiment as tightening continues, traders should be looking for opportunities to go long on bonds at higher yields, Mahajan adds.(bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0729 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/SEK declined Wednesday with bids entering the market possibly ahead of the general election Sept. 19, says SEB. The broader support should "cushion the decline and a bounce back towards the 9.27/28 area should not be too big a surprise," the bank adds. EUR/SEK trades at 9.2237. (charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0728 GMT [Dow Jones] India's RBI "still has work to do" when it comes to tempering domestic prices pressures, says \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 , Senior Emerging Markets Strategist, Brian Jackson. "WPI data (for August) shows inflation is moving in the right direction, but it is doing so slowly and upside risks remain present. We expect further policy action in the months ahead," Jackson says. Expects another 25 basis points hike in key lending rate by end-March. India's headline inflation for August grew 9.5%, slower than July's 9.6% but relatively high. Thursday's data also showed that primary articles rose 0.2% in the week to Sept. 4, while on-year it grew 16.22%. Food articles index rose 0.3% in week, while on-year it was up 15.10%. Fuel products index fell 0.4% in-week and rose 11.48% on-year. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0728 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  expects France's EUR7.5 billion to EUR8.5 billion auction of 2012, 2013 and 2015-dated BTAN government bonds Thursday to go well. Apart from the redemption flow from France this week, analyst Elaine Lin notes the "particularly attractive" relative valuation of the BTAN 0.75% September 2012, and adds that the BTAN 2.00% July 2015 has potential to continue to outperform vs the 2-year and/or 10-year part of the curve. Auction results are due at 0850 GMT. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0724 GMT [Dow Jones] The BOJ's massive USD/JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen Wednesday, some estimates put the total at around JPY2 trillion seems to have scared off the sellers for now. The spot traded in 85.20-75 range overnight and PM Kan backed up the action Thursday by saying he would not tolerate yen volatility. USD/JPY now trades at 85.40, market consensus seen to be that any dips toward 85.00 will likely attract the BOJ's attention. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0723 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  envisages a "reasonable outcome" at Spain's EUR3 billion to EUR4 billion auction of the 4.85% Oct 2020 and 4.70% July 2041 bonds Thursday. "While we would not be surprised" to see a little more cheapening into the supply, current valuations seem enough to ensure a reasonable outcome this morning," strategist Moyeen Islam says. Separately, Natixis says Spanish government bonds are very attractive in terms of valuation but remain "particularly risky". Natixis adds it prefers to steer clear of arbitrage trades against triple-A paper given the greater volatility displayed by core-peripheral spreads. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0721 GMT [Dow Jones] Desperate times call for desperate measures and we have just seen them with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Nicholas Hastings in \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Focus. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or really the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , probably felt it had no choice. The USD's decline against the JPY had got to the point where it was causing not only economic but political damage and something had to be done. By launching what will probably be confirmed as the country's largest ever \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  exercise, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has succeeded in pushing the USD back up from just under Y83 to somewhere between Y85-86, where it remains for now. But, JPY bulls should hang on. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0715 GMT [Dow Jones] RBI rate action will narrow corridor between key lending and borrowing rates to 100 bps from 125 bps but may not impact much for now, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  economist Rahul Bajoria. RBI wants "to reduce volatility in the overnight call money market but in the near term it's unlikely to have any significant market impact because liquidity is expected to remain tight going forward" given outflow of roughly INR450 billion for advance tax payments. Rate action indicates "growth remains on a very steady path" with not many upside or downside risks. Thinks RBI will not hike rates anymore until end-March; "the momentum of inflation has been broken...and there is little incentive for hiking aggressively". (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 0342ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g00058\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
