\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: STI Off 0.4%, Likely End Down; China Fall Weighs\b0\par\par\uc2 1,610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 08:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0643 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares expected to end lower, with initial gains wiped out by caution fuelled partly by weakness in China bourses, where stocks hurt by concerns over prospect of PBOC rate hike, additional tightening measures for Chinese banks. STI off 0.4% at 3057.72 vs new 2010 high of 3086.28 in early trade, with support tipped at 3043 August peak. "The optimism (in the morning) was fairly short-lived," says OCBC Investment Research analyst Philip Teo; "the index is likely to maintain its corrective stance" for rest of day. Decliners outnumbering gainers by almost 2 to 1 in broad market. Key decliners among STI components include Olam (O32.SG), off 2.3% at S$2.95, Noble Group (N21.SG), off 1.7% at S$1.76, DBS (D05.SG), off 1.0% at S$13.92, UOB (U11.SG), off 1.1% at S$18.54. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0640 GMT [Dow Jones] Indian government bonds turn down as RBI raises reverse repurchase rate by 50 basis points -- more than expected -- while also hiking repo rate by 25 basis points, disappointing some sections of market who had hoped for 25 basis point hike in both key rates; 7.80% 2020 government bond at 98.81 vs 99.17 before announcement, 99.02 last close. Still, market may find support at current levels as RBI also signals path towards normalising policy rates may be nearly over, says trading head of private bank. Tips bond yields to face resistance at 8.00%, now 7.97%. 10 out of 18 economists polled by Dow Jones expected RBI to raise both policy rates by 25 basis points each. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0638 GMT [Dow Jones] Kingfisher's (KGF.LN) 1H results show adjusted pretax profit up by 23%, largely due to self-help initiatives says an analyst. This is decent, but the analyst thinks the market will focus on management's comments regarding the consumer environment. There are concerns about the fragility of the consumer market and it looks like bottom- line growth will be driven by margin improvements. Shares closed Wednesday at 218.9p. (andrea.tryphonides@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0636 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds open lower, catching up with post-settlement declines of Wednesday ahead of heavy supply later this session. On offer: EUR4B worth of Spanish paper and French nominal bonds and linkers worth up to EUR10.3B. The former auction seen as a key test of appetite for peripherals. No major euro zone data due Thursday, although US jobless claims come at 1230 GMT and Philadelphia Fed survey at 1400 GMT. Trading's likely to remain volatile. "The market appears to be having difficulties reconciling prevailing levels against evolving fundamentals and this is not something we see changing in the near future," says Nomura. December bunds down 0.22 at 129.89 10-year bund down at 98.48 yielding 2.424%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0636 GMT [Dow Jones] RBI surprises analysts by raising key borrowing rate 50 bps; as expected, lifts key lending rate 25 bps as it seeks to temper price pressures in rapidly growing economy. Ups repurchase rate to 6.00%, reverse repo to 5.00%. Move has backing of government as finance minister said Thursday he wasn't satisfied with current level of inflation. But rate hike cycle may be close to over; "the RBI believes the tightening that has been carried out over this period has taken the monetary situation close to normal," bank says in statement. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0632 GMT [Dow Jones] The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance to curb the franc's advance cannot be compared with the Bank of Japan's action Wednesday to check the yen's gains, according to \uc2 UBS\uc2 . "The market took on the SNB with good reason as its economy was stronger than those in the euro region and it has a much stronger fiscal position," says strategist Greg Gibbs. By contrast "Japan's economy is suffering, its stock market is underperforming and its mired in deflation," he says. EUR/CHF at 1.3013 from a low of 1.2987. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0629 GMT [Dow Jones] While BOJ seeming to leave JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilized could help weaken JPY, what Japanese authorities really need is stronger recovery in U.S. economy, says Yuji Kameoka, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Daiwa Institute of Research\uc2 . "There could be some yen weakening effect given that the Bank of Japan is increasing yen liquidity supply by (so far) not sterilizing the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds," Kameoka says. But intermittent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could take time to lead to more JPY weakness; better fix would be stronger U.S. economy. "If the U.S. economic outlook improves, pushing up U.S. interest rates, a fundamental shift toward yen weakness and dollar strength would not be far off."(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0626 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly lower; Nikkei flat, S&P/ASX 200 off 1.2%, Kospi down 0.7%, Shanghai Composite 2.3% lower, HSI fell 0.5%, Taiex off 0.8%, STI down 0.3%, SET +0.4%, NZX-50 flat. Malaysia markets shut for holiday. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, JPY gained tad vs EUR, USD as Japanese authorities appeared to stay out of the markets today after they stepped in to weaken JPY sharply Wednesday. USD/JPY at 85.31 vs 85.63 late NY trade Wednesday; EUR/JPY at 110.80 vs 111.36. EUR/USD at 1.2988 vs 1.3012. BOJ Governor Shirakawa says unconventional monetary easing measures were "highly effective" against "acute pains" in recent financial crisis. BOJ pumped liquidity into banking system, in sign it is trying to help government weaken JPY by leaving cash from Wednesday's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in money market; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  injected Y1 trillion into system vs Y700 billion infusion market expected, after skipping its morning \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Japan July Tertiary Industry Activity Index +1.6% on-month seasonally-adjusted to 98.9 vs +0.2% in June. Bloomberg, citing person familiar with matter, reported that China Banking Regulatory Commission may require country's biggest lenders to boost their capital adequacy ratio to as high as 15% by end-2012 vs 11.5% currently. Australian August merchandise imports fell to A$17.58 billion seasonally-adjusted vs A$18.80 billion in July. Thai Industries Sentiment Index fell to 102.4 points in August vs 108.6 points in July. RBNZ keeps official cash rate steady at 3.0%, highlighting ongoing economic uncertainty in light of recent 7.0 magnitude earthquake in Christchurch. Spot gold at $1,265.50 per troy ounce, down $2.70 from NY Wednesday. October crude oil futures down $1.00 at $75.02/bbl. (wei-zhe.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0618 GMT [Dow Jones] December bunds are down 0.25 at 129.86, and WestLB says the technical picture remains fragile. The bank sees crucial support at 129.53, the low from Sept. 13, which shields the Aug. 11 low at 129.01. Resistance is at 130.27, 130.41, and 130.56. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0616 GMT [Dow Jones] India interbank call rate, collateralized lending and borrowing obligation rate rises above RBI's key lending rate of 5.75% on expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may lift key rates at policy meeting (due 0630GMT), says state-run bank trader. Overnight call rate at 6.00% vs 5.75% last close, CBLO rate at 5.78% vs 5.75% at open. Trader says, banks also borrowing heavily as corporates withdraw funds on last day of advance tax payment Thursday. "The market is pricing in at least a 25 basis points rate hike and lenders have positioned themselves accordingly. If RBI does lift rates, we can see the call rate touching 6.25% in session," trader says. Notes, banks also borrowing heavily from RBI's daily discount lending window. Tips call rate in 5.75%-6.25% band in session. Indian banks borrowed INR518.50 billion from RBI Thursday. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0614 GMT [Dow Jones] The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  is widely expected to keep its key interest rate on hold Thursday, with market players focusing instead on its outlooks for growth and inflation, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . "The recent franc gains may provoke renewed SNB concerns on deflation," says Lutz Karpowitz. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday to check yen gains has stirred speculation of similar SNB actions, but \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  notes the two scenarios cannot be compared as the Swiss economy is booming and the risks of deflation have receded. "Should the SNB raise the issue of deflation however, this could weigh on the Swiss franc," he notes. EUR/CHF at 1.3008 from a previous close of 1.3042.(neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0612 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's economy is more dependent on China than ever, and little can be done to guard against uncertainties of that connection, says Philip Lowe, Assistant Governor of RBA in afternoon speech. "Clearly what happens in the Australian economy is now more dependent upon what happens in China than has been the case at any time in our past," he says. Still, assistant governor says improving China's data collection and dissemination process was key, signalling Australia's statistics bureau and central were looking to help in that process. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 0243ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g00048\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
