Aug. 11 low at 129.01. Resistance is at 130.27, 130.41, and 130.56. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0616 GMT [Dow Jones] India interbank call rate, collateralized lending and borrowing obligation rate rises above RBI's key lending rate of 5.75% on expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may lift key rates at policy meeting (due 0630GMT), says state-run bank trader. Overnight call rate at 6.00% vs 5.75% last close, CBLO rate at 5.78% vs 5.75% at open. Trader says, banks also borrowing heavily as corporates withdraw funds on last day of advance tax payment Thursday. "The market is pricing in at least a 25 basis points rate hike and lenders have positioned themselves accordingly. If RBI does lift rates, we can see the call rate touching 6.25% in session," trader says. Notes, banks also borrowing heavily from RBI's daily discount lending window. Tips call rate in 5.75%-6.25% band in session. Indian banks borrowed INR518.50 billion from RBI Thursday. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0614 GMT [Dow Jones] The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  is widely expected to keep its key interest rate on hold Thursday, with market players focusing instead on its outlooks for growth and inflation, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . "The recent franc gains may provoke renewed SNB concerns on deflation," says Lutz Karpowitz. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday to check yen gains has stirred speculation of similar SNB actions, but \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  notes the two scenarios cannot be compared as the Swiss economy is booming and the risks of deflation have receded. "Should the SNB raise the issue of deflation however, this could weigh on the Swiss franc," he notes. EUR/CHF at 1.3008 from a previous close of 1.3042.(neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0612 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's economy is more dependent on China than ever, and little can be done to guard against uncertainties of that connection, says Philip Lowe, Assistant Governor of RBA in afternoon speech. "Clearly what happens in the Australian economy is now more dependent upon what happens in China than has been the case at any time in our past," he says. Still, assistant governor says improving China's data collection and dissemination process was key, signalling Australia's statistics bureau and central were looking to help in that process. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0610 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia government bonds extend winning streak to 2 sessions as foreign investors continue to buy after week-long holiday, traders say. "Trade volume is lighter compared with yesterday but foreign funds are still driving prices higher," trader says. Still, "yields, especially for the 10- and 15-year bonds, may rise tomorrow ahead of the finance ministry's bond auction (next) Tuesday." 5-year yield steady at 7.38%, 10-year yield at 7.91% vs 7.93% yesterday; 15-year at 8.45% vs 8.51%; 20-year at 8.88% vs 8.98%; 28-year at 9.18% vs 9.26%. Finance ministry plans to sell IDR3 trillion worth of papers maturing in 1-, 11-, 16-, and 21-year on Sept. 21.(andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0604 GMT [Dow Jones] The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  is likely to "play it safe" Thursday, and continue with its low interest rate policy due to the strong franc and the danger of a slowdown in global economic growth, says \uc2 Bank Sarasin\uc2 . "Swiss exports have weathered the franc's appreciation surprising well, the risk of a second global recession entails the danger of a further contraction in the Swiss economy, and with very low price pressure it can err on the side of expansive rather than tight policy," economist Alessandro Bee says. The SNB decision on rates is due at 1200 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 0238ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g00046\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184