\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:US Recovery Would Weaken JPY More Than BOJ - Daiwa\b0\par\par\uc2 1,647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 08:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0629 GMT [Dow Jones] While BOJ seeming to leave JPY-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unsterilized could help weaken JPY, what Japanese authorities really need is stronger recovery in U.S. economy, says Yuji Kameoka, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daiwa Institute of Research\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "There could be some yen weakening effect given that the Bank of Japan is increasing yen liquidity supply by (so far) not sterilizing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  funds," Kameoka says. But intermittent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could take time to lead to more JPY weakness; better fix would be stronger U.S. economy. "If the U.S. economic outlook improves, pushing up U.S. interest rates, a fundamental shift toward yen weakness and dollar strength would not be far off."(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0626 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly lower; Nikkei flat, S&P/ASX 200 off 1.2%, Kospi down 0.7%, Shanghai Composite 2.3% lower, HSI fell 0.5%, Taiex off 0.8%, STI down 0.3%, SET +0.4%, NZX-50 flat. Malaysia markets shut for holiday. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, JPY gained tad vs EUR, USD as Japanese authorities appeared to stay out of the markets today after they stepped in to weaken JPY sharply Wednesday. USD/JPY at 85.31 vs 85.63 late NY trade Wednesday; EUR/JPY at 110.80 vs 111.36. EUR/USD at 1.2988 vs 1.3012. BOJ Governor Shirakawa says unconventional monetary easing measures were "highly effective" against "acute pains" in recent financial crisis. BOJ pumped liquidity into banking system, in sign it is trying to help government weaken JPY by leaving cash from Wednesday's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in money market; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  injected Y1 trillion into system vs Y700 billion infusion market expected, after skipping its morning \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Japan July Tertiary Industry Activity Index +1.6% on-month seasonally-adjusted to 98.9 vs +0.2% in June. Bloomberg, citing person familiar with matter, reported that China Banking Regulatory Commission may require country's biggest lenders to boost their capital adequacy ratio to as high as 15% by end-2012 vs 11.5% currently. Australian August merchandise imports fell to A$17.58 billion seasonally-adjusted vs A$18.80 billion in July. Thai Industries Sentiment Index fell to 102.4 points in August vs 108.6 points in July. RBNZ keeps official cash rate steady at 3.0%, highlighting ongoing economic uncertainty in light of recent 7.0 magnitude earthquake in Christchurch. Spot gold at $1,265.50 per troy ounce, down $2.70 from NY Wednesday. October crude oil futures down $1.00 at $75.02/bbl. (wei-zhe.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0618 GMT [Dow Jones] December bunds are down 0.25 at 129.86, and WestLB says the technical picture remains fragile. The bank sees crucial support at 129.53, the low from Sept. 13, which shields the Aug. 11 low at 129.01. Resistance is at 130.27, 130.41, and 130.56. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0616 GMT [Dow Jones] India interbank call rate, collateralized lending and borrowing obligation rate rises above RBI's key lending rate of 5.75% on expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may lift key rates at policy meeting (due 0630GMT), says state-run bank trader. Overnight call rate at 6.00% vs 5.75% last close, CBLO rate at 5.78% vs 5.75% at open. Trader says, banks also borrowing heavily as corporates withdraw funds on last day of advance tax payment Thursday. "The market is pricing in at least a 25 basis points rate hike and lenders have positioned themselves accordingly. If RBI does lift rates, we can see the call rate touching 6.25% in session," trader says. Notes, banks also borrowing heavily from RBI's daily discount lending window. Tips call rate in 5.75%-6.25% band in session. Indian banks borrowed INR518.50 billion from RBI Thursday. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0614 GMT [Dow Jones] The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  is widely expected to keep its key interest rate on hold Thursday, with market players focusing instead on its outlooks for growth and inflation, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . "The recent franc gains may provoke renewed SNB concerns on deflation," says Lutz Karpowitz. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday to check yen gains has stirred speculation of similar SNB actions, but \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  notes the two scenarios cannot be compared as the Swiss economy is booming and the risks of deflation have receded. "Should the SNB raise the issue of deflation however, this could weigh on the Swiss franc," he notes. EUR/CHF at 1.3008 from a previous close of 1.3042.(neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0612 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's economy is more dependent on China than ever, and little can be done to guard against uncertainties of that connection, says Philip Lowe, Assistant Governor of RBA in afternoon speech. "Clearly what happens in the Australian economy is now more dependent upon what happens in China than has been the case at any time in our past," he says. Still, assistant governor says improving China's data collection and dissemination process was key, signalling Australia's statistics bureau and central were looking to help in that process. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0610 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia government bonds extend winning streak to 2 sessions as foreign investors continue to buy after week-long holiday, traders say. "Trade volume is lighter compared with yesterday but foreign funds are still driving prices higher," trader says. Still, "yields, especially for the 10- and 15-year bonds, may rise tomorrow ahead of the finance ministry's bond auction (next) Tuesday." 5-year yield steady at 7.38%, 10-year yield at 7.91% vs 7.93% yesterday; 15-year at 8.45% vs 8.51%; 20-year at 8.88% vs 8.98%; 28-year at 9.18% vs 9.26%. Finance ministry plans to sell IDR3 trillion worth of papers maturing in 1-, 11-, 16-, and 21-year on Sept. 21.(andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0604 GMT [Dow Jones] The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  is likely to "play it safe" Thursday, and continue with its low interest rate policy due to the strong franc and the danger of a slowdown in global economic growth, says \uc2 Bank Sarasin\uc2 . "Swiss exports have weathered the franc's appreciation surprising well, the risk of a second global recession entails the danger of a further contraction in the Swiss economy, and with very low price pressure it can err on the side of expansive rather than tight policy," economist Alessandro Bee says. The SNB decision on rates is due at 1200 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0552 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares extend losses in moderate volume midday on profit-taking; benchmark index last down 0.8% at 3,329.749, though off intraday low of 3,323.646. "Market is biased down due to profit-taking after yesterday's gains (+3.9%)," dealer says. Among decliners, \uc2 Astra International\uc2  (ASII.JK) down 5.1% at IDR54,850; \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) down 2.2% at IDR6,600; \uc2 Indofood\uc2  (INDF.JK) down 1.9% at IDR5,050. 3,250-3,360 range tipped for the rest of the session. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0552 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 December futures flat at 9470, trading in and out of negative territory as investors staying on sidelines after index moved above 9500 (peaking at 9580 early in session); buying cues exhausted after \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , investors waiting to see if government will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  further, say analysts. "Since it (the futures index) gained sharply yesterday, investors may not chase the market higher unless there is a surprise," says Japanese brokerage manager, adding that afternoon's low trading volume reflects caution. Says index may hover around 9500 in coming days if no further action from government is seen.(ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0550 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares fall after midday break, as banks sink further on continued worries over possibility of rate hike or capital requirement tightening. Shanghai Composite Index last down 2.0% at 2598.52, tipped to test support at 2550. "Blue chips rarely fall like this, and they aren't likely to stay down for long. The market is anticipating a rate hike, and if Beijing doesn't announce changes to interest rates by this weekend, the market may rally sharply next week," says Simon Wang at \uc2 Guoyuan Securities\uc2 . Adds, Shanghai index may see more losses until then. Agricultural Bank of China (601288.SH) still leading losers; stock down 2.2% at CNY2.62. ICBC (601398.SH) down 1.0% at CNY3.99. Shenzhen Composite Index down 2.6% at 1151.76. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0550 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.4% at 924.72 midday in moderate trade, with property stocks taking off on anticipation BOT may hold rates steady at October meeting after Governor Tarisa says yesterday it has no immediate plans to impose new measures to curb THB. But still, some caution in market, with KGI Securities strategist Rakpong Chaisuparakul saying investors are awaiting outcome of court challenge seeking injunction against next week's 3G mobile license auction; adds sentiment will improve if auction cleared to go ahead. Resistance at 930, support at 920. Among top actives, Land & Houses (LH.TH) +6.1% at THB6.95, Asian Property Development (AP.TH) +2.7% at THB7.70, Quality Houses (QH.TH) +1.6% at THB2.62, PTT (PTT.TH) +0.7% at THB283, CP All (CPALL.TH) +2.6% at THB39, \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2  (KBANK.TH) +1.4% at THB106.50. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 0229ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g00041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
