\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Kospi Down 0.4%; 1810 Support Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,779 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 06:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0450 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi slips further, off 0.4% at 1815.93 amid profit-taking ahead of Chuseok holidays from Sept. 21 to 23. \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2  analyst Bae Sung-young says market seeing sustained selling from institutional and retail investors: "The Chuseok holidays are a bit long, so that may be playing a psychological factor. Chinese markets are also extending losses." But adds, continued support from foreign investors will limit the degree of decline; tips 1810 support. Firms seen dependent on Chinese demand suffering from worries about future demand there amid growing concerns Beijing may raise rates; \uc2 LG Chem\uc2  (051910.SE) 4.7% lower at KRW322,000, Posco (005490.SE) off 2.9% at KRW509,000. Automakers also weaker on worries that Japanese government's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday to lift USD/JPY may weaken their competitive edge against Japanese rivals; \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) 2.5% lower at KRW154,000, \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) down 1.5% at KRW34,100. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0444 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY stays down but stabilizes around 6.7330 midday on USD demand from Chinese banks, traders say; in OTC trade, USD/CNY at 6.7337 vs 6.7422 last close, intraday low of 6.7270; "I don't believe the dollar will fall below CNY6.7000 in the near term," says Shanghai-based trader at an European bank. "Either from economic policy or political pressure considerations, the over 1% yuan appreciation since early September is already very big." USD/CNY tipped to consolidate in 6.7250-6.7400 rest of session. Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY NDF at 6.6500/6.6550 vs 6.6646/6.6686 late yesterday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks surrender initial gains as investors offload on strength amid pullback in most Asian bourses. STI off 0.2% at 3065.68 midday after setting new 2010 high of 3086.28 in early trade; support expected at 3043 August peak. Market breadth negative at 1.5 decliners for every gainer vs 2 gainers for each decliner earlier. "We would be cautious to enter positions with markets being choppy," says SIAS Research technical analyst Edmund Seow; "if we do enter, we would enter small and then build the position up." Besides Genting group, most other top-volume stocks are small caps, including Transcu (E15.SG), +8.7% at S$0.125, \uc2 United Fiber System\uc2  (P30.SG), flat at S$0.06, Healthway Medical (5NG.SG), +5.9% at S$0.18, \uc2 Hisaka Holdings\uc2  (FV2.SG), +10.0% at S$0.385. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] Macquarie puts 12-month Topix target at 980 (15X house's FY ending March 2012 expected EPS of 65). "At 980, the Topix would discount what we expect to be a slow saucer-shaped recovery in Topix EPS back towards the FYs ending 2006-08 high plateau of 70-75. We believe Topix EPS will return to 70 in FY ending March 2013 (after five lost years)." At 980, notes Topix would trade at 14X EPS of 70, vs 15X expected level for global equities to continue to trade. Topix currently at 844.12, down 7.0% year-to-date. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI finally snaps 5-day winning streak, but "holding up pretty well" in face of slew of fund-raising activities earlier this week, then China banks' fall today, says futures trader. HSI down 0.5% at 21,627.25 midday, weighed in most part by China banks: ICBC (1398.HK) down 1.2% at HK$5.84, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) down 1.5% at HK$6.64, Bank of China (3988.HK) down 0.5% at HK$4.03, Bank of Communications (3328.HK) down 1.1% at HK$8.45 -- these 4 stocks responsible for 44% of HSI's 98-point drop midday, hurt by Bloomberg report CBRC may require biggest lenders to boost CAR to as high as 15% by end-2012. Bucking downtrend, Cosco Pacific (1199.HK) +5.6% at HK$11.28 after \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  adds stock to Conviction Buy list. Market volume solid at HK$37.63 billion midday. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures up 0.21 at 142.33 whereas 10-year bond yield unchanged at 1.040%; RBS rates strategist RuiXue Xu says demand from investors such as banks for short- to medium-term bonds remains strong; they're willing to buy assets on any dips. Adds since Japan MOF acted in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, "now it's more likely that the government will put more pressure on the BOJ to ease its policy further" at next scheduled policy meeting, another tailwind for JGBs, she says. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0427 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end +0.2% at 449.52 with investors coming back to buy on expectations government to outline measures to support stock market. "Some players are optimistic that the government will have to act soon because it said last week it would apply flexible monetary policy to stabilize the stock market after recent losses," says Hanoi-based brokerage analyst. Adds, buying interest is stronger today after key index has been unable to break technical support of 445 this week. Volume moderate with 38 million shares valued at VND989 billion exchanging hands. Among most active, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) ends +2.9% at VND35,000, Eximbank (EIB.VH) ends flat at VND17,300, Vinh Son Song Hinh Hydropower (VSH.VH) end down 0.8% at VND12,200. Traders predict more gains tomorrow with index support to range 450-455. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0425 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 +0.2% at 3200.52 as market buoyed by investors moving into yield stocks on back of pause in official cash rate; but benchmark index may lose some ground on offshore moves, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Belinda Stanley; "Australia (market) has slipped back slightly as financials come under pressure and we might see that push us back." Says investors today looking to buy in to high yield stock like Warehouse (WHS.NZ), +0.3% at NZ$3.75, \uc2 Contact Energy\uc2  (CEN.NZ), +0.7% at NZ$5.82, after RBNZ announced it would hold official cash rate at 3.0%. Says \uc2 Fisher & Paykel Appliances\uc2  (FPA.NZ), +5.2% at NZ$0.61, on continued strength following announcement yesterday had jointly developed a new refrigeration compressor; "That is where \uc2 Fisher & Paykel Appliances\uc2  are really a jump a head of their competition in the development and new technologies that they can bring to the market." Says \uc2 Pyne Gould Corp\uc2 . (PGC.NZ) +2.4% at NZ$2.42 on news wholly owned financial services company MARAC would merge with CBS Canterbury, Southern Cross Building Society. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares lower midday, led by banks amid concerns over possibility of Beijing raising interest rates, banks' capital adequacy requirements. Shanghai Composite Index down 1.5% at 2613.39, tipped to test immediate support at 2600, with 2550 as next target. "Agricultural Bank shares have been hovering around its IPO price since it listed. When they dropped below CNY2.68, it inspired selling in other banks," says Wang Liangxing at Western Securities. Adds, property developers paring losses on bargain hunting, may support index rest of session. Bloomberg reports today banking regulator considering raising biggest banks' CAR to 15%; that raising concerns in market banks may need fresh round of financing, analysts say. Agricultural Bank of China (601288.SH) down 2.2% at CNY2.62, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (601939.SH) down 1.5% at CNY4.54. \uc2 China Vanke\uc2  (000002.SZ) down 0.5% at CNY8.12 off earlier low of CNY8.07, \uc2 Poly Real Estate Group\uc2  (600048.SH) down 0.3% at CNY11.12 off earlier low of 11.07. Shenzhen Composite Index down 2.4% at 1153.66. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0417 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY could fall below 85.00 by end of global day, as apparent absence of any Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaves pair prone to selling by Japanese exporters, other players taking profits, says senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at major Japanese bank. After U.S. Congressman Levin, chair of House Ways and Means Committee, said overnight \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "deeply disturbing," trader says any more negative comments from U.S. officials would weigh further on pair. Investors will closely watch U.S. Treasury Secretary Geithner testimony before Senate committee at 1400 GMT, before House committee at 1800 GMT for any cues to thinking on Japan move. But dealer says most likely, Geithner will refrain from direct comment on Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF has likely received Treasury's assurance to refrain from direct criticism of Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/JPY last at 85.28. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0414 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds tad down as markets await \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's mid-quarter policy announcement, says dealer with private bank; 7.80% 2020 benchmark bond at 99.00 vs 99.02 Wednesday close. "The next move in the market will be only after the policy. However, a 25 basis point hike is already factored in and may not lead to a major fall," says dealer. Adds, "the market will closely watch the tone of the policy. If a rate hike is accompanied by a hawkish statement, we may see a big fall in bond prices." Tips yield on 10-year paper in tight range of 7.94%-7.96%, last at 7.95%. Notes, yields may fall to 7.80% if RBI keeps both rates unchanged. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0410 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.1% at 9507.27, turning negative after positive morning performance as USD/JPY weakens (now 85.26). Futures selling dragging down cash market, says Hikaru Sato, senior technical analyst at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. "Investors want more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but there is a sense of disillusionment that the government may not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  today," he says. Analysts say if USD/JPY falls below 85, Nikkei may be supported at 9450, while if pair stays above 85 exporters may keep gains. \uc2 Mazda\uc2  (7261.TO) up 0.5% at Y202, \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) up 1.5% at Y2,637. Commodity stocks lower; Inpex (1605.TO) down 2.2% at Y402,500 on falling crude prices. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com) [ 09-16-10 0050ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g00034\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
