\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: EUR/JPY Falls; Japan Exporters Sell - Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,657 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 05:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0349 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY falls to 110.65 as Japan exporters' EUR sales triggered stop loss orders below 111.00, says senior dealer at major Japan trust bank. USD/JPY falls to 85.26, EUR/USD to 1.2975 in tandem. Says support for EUR/JPY up to how far dollar/yen falls, with USD/JPY being closely watched by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities who may embark on another JPY selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Dealer declines to guess what level \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at, but notes 85.00 may be key. Aside from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's potential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , market's focus on exporter USD, EUR sales. EUR/JPY last 85.27, EUR/JPY last 110.75, EUR/USD last 1.2989.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0335 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares trade lower with banks leading declines amid growing concerns about possibility of PBOC interest rate hike, further tightening measures. Agricultural Bank of China (601288.SH) is sector's biggest loser; stock last down 2.2% at CNY2.62, falling below CNY2.68 IPO price for first time. Shanghai Composite Index down 1.8% at 2603.76, tipped to test immediate support at 2600, with 2550 as next target. "The rate hike worries seem to be gathering momentum the longer PBOC maintains its silence," says Li Xin at China Development Bank Securities. "At this point, if PBOC unveils a rate hike, it might actually have a positive effect on the market." \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  down 2.0% at CNY4.52. Shenzhen Composite Index down 2.4% at 1153.66. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0334 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.2% at 923.21 after BOT governor Wednesday says there are no immediate plan to impose measures to curb THB's rise; such worries had put SET index under pressure in past few days as THB weakness would deter foreign investment in market. Weakness in regional bourses likely capped gains, says analyst with Finansia Syrus Securities Somchai Anektaweepon. "Capital inflows are expected to continue flooding into Asian markets including Thailand, so the Thai stock market will tend to go up," he adds. Investors watching if court will issue injunction to block 3G license auction set to kick off next week. Resistance tipped at 925-926, support at 915-910. Among top actives, Land & Houses (LH.TH) +3.8% at THB6.80, Banpu (BANPU.TH) +0.3% at THB644, PTT (PTT.TH) 0.4% at THB282, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) flat at tHB92.50, CP All (CPALL.TH) +2.6% at THB39. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0327 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds mixed in mild trade, with many players holding off as they wait for fresh cues. Participants still skeptical over potential imposition of measures to stem THB strength despite BOT Governor's comment yesterday bank has no immediate plan to take additional steps to curb THB's rise. Such worries pushed up yields in recent sessions. "Most players are waiting to see a clearer picture to be sure that such measures really are not underway," says trader. Yields expected to move within 5 bps range either way today. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.74%/2.68% vs 2.78%/2.72% late yesterday, November 2016 quoted at 2.87%/2.80% vs 2.90%/2.80%, June 2019 quoted at 3.05%/2.95% vs 2.98%/2.92%. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0327 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options drop sharply to 11.10%/11.70% vs 11.80%/12.50% New York Wednesday, as spot floats around 85.50 in Asia morning without sharp moves, reducing demand for hedges against fluctuations, such as those on Wednesday when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets. Options dealer at major \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  bank says view growing that Japan authorities will continue to trim recent JPY strength, further reducing demand for hedging against pair's falls. Dealer says if spot stays around current 85.40-85.50 levels in global day, vols may decline 20 bps; if spot rise gradually, vols may decline toward 10.5% in near term. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0324 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY now at 85.27, may fall below 85.00 due to USD, EUR sales by Japanese exporters if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities do not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in market, says Masafumi Yamamoto, Barclays Bank Chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But notes that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  whenever they see opportunity to take market by surprise and will not wait for yen to hit fixed levels to prevent JPY strengthening. Markets focused on whether or not BOJ will continue to leave funds in market instead of "sterilizing" through money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  as is usually done. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refrained from any draining of funds in morning \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  window. Focus now on 0400 GMT money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Leaving funds would effectively add to monetary base and further monetary easing. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0322 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares down 0.4% at 3344.230 with losses in heavyweight \uc2 Astra International\uc2  (ASII.JK) weighing on main index, dealers say; market breadth positive though with gainers leading decliners 69 to 62. Volume healthy with 854 billion shares worth IDR1.1 trillion exchanging hands. "Investors are still buying stocks, but they are selective in their investments after strong gains yesterday," dealer says. Astra down 2.8% at IDR56,250, while its CPO unit Astra Agro (ALLI.JK) down 1.4% at IDR20,650; cigarette maker Gudan Garam (GGRM.JK) down 2.3% at IDR4,500; Telkom (TLKM.JK) +0.5% at IDR9,300. 3,270-3,390 range tipped for main index today. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0316 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR likely to open tad up, tracking broad weakness in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , likely lower opening for local stocks, says dealer with foreign bank; pair likely to open around 46.37-46.40 vs 46.36 in late trade yesterday. "Importers around Asia are buying dollars to cover up on near-term payments after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they expect other central banks to follow suit, which should help the pair to rise," says dealer. Notes RBI's rate decision unlikely to have much impact on pair, only stock moves expected to have impact. Tips pair in 46.30-46.50 band in session. Foreign funds net buyers of provisional INR22.15 billion worth of stocks yesterday, USD/INR 1-month NDFs at 46.47/46.54. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0314 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex likely to open 0.3%-0.4% lower, tracking losses in most regional markets, says local dealer. Adds, stretched valuations in most index stocks after over 17% rally since June 9 may weigh, while investors cautious before RBI rate-setting meeting due 0630 GMT. Still, thinks Sensex could find support at 19,350 as liquidity remains strong on the back of sustained foreign inflows. Foreign funds were net buyers yesterday, picking shares worth provisional INR22.15 billion Wednesday, taking total inflows to about INR66 billion in last 3 sessions. 10 of 18 economists polled by Dow Jones expect RBI to hike repo, reverse repo rates by 25 bps, while 4 expect 25 bp hike in only reverse repo. Sensex ended +0.8% at 19,502.11 Wednesday. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0310 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD rangebound in quiet trade as players still figuring out implication of (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's) JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says senior trader at Singapore bank. Tips pair in 7.7660-7.7690 band today. "Players are not very active today and I've spotted a local bank selling about US$50 million at HK$7.7665. The transaction amount aren't sizable with around US$20 million to US$30 million on average per deal," trader says. Adds, lower USD/CNY fixing continues to cap USD/HKD rise. Pair last at 7.7671 vs 7.7675 this morning vs 7.7666 yesterday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0259 GMT [Dow Jones] Indian government bonds likely to open lower tracking fall in longer-dated U.S. Treasurys, then tread water ahead of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate decision due 0630 GMT, says trader with primary dealership. Adds "the market has broadly factored in a 25-basis points hike in both rates. If the RBI decides to raise only the reverse repo rate or in fact pause, we will see a rally." Standard Chartered Bank says that even if a 25bp hike on both rates happens, "in the near term, we expect the rise in 10-year government bond yield to be capped at 8% on the back of investor demand and a dearth of supply (only one auction of INR110 billion remaining in September)." 10 out of 18 economists polled by Dow Jones expect a hike in both rates, 4 expect a hike in reverse repo only. Dealer tips 10-year paper in 7.95%-7.98% band (7.95% last close) till rate decision. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0256 GMT [Dow Jones] Chances of U.S. Congress taking punitive action against China on CNY valuation issue never been higher, says Standard Chartered; "with the mid-term elections looking bad for the (U.S.) Administration, a measured bill on this issue...might, they decide, be a price worth paying." Adds if proposed bill classifying CNY undervaluation as subsidy, U.S. would slap punitive tariff on imports from China. But says China could, in turn, punish big U.S. companies in China, file case with WTO. "This would take two, possibly three, years to wind its way through the dispute settlement process"; adds unclear how WTO would rule over such case. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 2348ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g0002i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
