 or not BOJ will continue to leave funds in market instead of "sterilizing" through money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  as is usually done. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refrained from any draining of funds in morning \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  window. Focus now on 0400 GMT money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Leaving funds would effectively add to monetary base and further monetary easing. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0322 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares down 0.4% at 3344.230 with losses in heavyweight \uc2 Astra International\uc2  (ASII.JK) weighing on main index, dealers say; market breadth positive though with gainers leading decliners 69 to 62. Volume healthy with 854 billion shares worth IDR1.1 trillion exchanging hands. "Investors are still buying stocks, but they are selective in their investments after strong gains yesterday," dealer says. Astra down 2.8% at IDR56,250, while its CPO unit Astra Agro (ALLI.JK) down 1.4% at IDR20,650; cigarette maker Gudan Garam (GGRM.JK) down 2.3% at IDR4,500; Telkom (TLKM.JK) +0.5% at IDR9,300. 3,270-3,390 range tipped for main index today. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0316 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR likely to open tad up, tracking broad weakness in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , likely lower opening for local stocks, says dealer with foreign bank; pair likely to open around 46.37-46.40 vs 46.36 in late trade yesterday. "Importers around Asia are buying dollars to cover up on near-term payments after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they expect other central banks to follow suit, which should help the pair to rise," says dealer. Notes RBI's rate decision unlikely to have much impact on pair, only stock moves expected to have impact. Tips pair in 46.30-46.50 band in session. Foreign funds net buyers of provisional INR22.15 billion worth of stocks yesterday, USD/INR 1-month NDFs at 46.47/46.54. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0314 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex likely to open 0.3%-0.4% lower, tracking losses in most regional markets, says local dealer. Adds, stretched valuations in most index stocks after over 17% rally since June 9 may weigh, while investors cautious before RBI rate-setting meeting due 0630 GMT. Still, thinks Sensex could find support at 19,350 as liquidity remains strong on the back of sustained foreign inflows. Foreign funds were net buyers yesterday, picking shares worth provisional INR22.15 billion Wednesday, taking total inflows to about INR66 billion in last 3 sessions. 10 of 18 economists polled by Dow Jones expect RBI to hike repo, reverse repo rates by 25 bps, while 4 expect 25 bp hike in only reverse repo. Sensex ended +0.8% at 19,502.11 Wednesday. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0310 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD rangebound in quiet trade as players still figuring out implication of (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's) JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says senior trader at Singapore bank. Tips pair in 7.7660-7.7690 band today. "Players are not very active today and I've spotted a local bank selling about US$50 million at HK$7.7665. The transaction amount aren't sizable with around US$20 million to US$30 million on average per deal," trader says. Adds, lower USD/CNY fixing continues to cap USD/HKD rise. Pair last at 7.7671 vs 7.7675 this morning vs 7.7666 yesterday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0259 GMT [Dow Jones] Indian government bonds likely to open lower tracking fall in longer-dated U.S. Treasurys, then tread water ahead of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate decision due 0630 GMT, says trader with primary dealership. Adds "the market has broadly factored in a 25-basis points hike in both rates. If the RBI decides to raise only the reverse repo rate or in fact pause, we will see a rally." Standard Chartered Bank says that even if a 25bp hike on both rates happens, "in the near term, we expect the rise in 10-year government bond yield to be capped at 8% on the back of investor demand and a dearth of supply (only one auction of INR110 billion remaining in September)." 10 out of 18 economists polled by Dow Jones expect a hike in both rates, 4 expect a hike in reverse repo only. Dealer tips 10-year paper in 7.95%-7.98% band (7.95% last close) till rate decision. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0256 GMT [Dow Jones] Chances of U.S. Congress taking punitive action against China on CNY valuation issue never been higher, says Standard Chartered; "with the mid-term elections looking bad for the (U.S.) Administration, a measured bill on this issue...might, they decide, be a price worth paying." Adds if proposed bill classifying CNY undervaluation as subsidy, U.S. would slap punitive tariff on imports from China. But says China could, in turn, punish big U.S. companies in China, file case with WTO. "This would take two, possibly three, years to wind its way through the dispute settlement process"; adds unclear how WTO would rule over such case. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0254 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiex off 0.4% early at 8136.88 as market gets jitters at key 8200 resistance, says Capital Securities analyst Alan Tseng. "Economic uncertainty across the globe has made investors tentative today," he says. Adds solar sector among biggest losers after Gintech Energy (3514.TW) president Wenwhe Pan says global demand in solar cells will weaken in November. Tips 8100-8200 range. Gintech Energy off 5.7% at NT$94.80, Sino American Silicon Products (5483.TW) down 6.9% at NT$99.60, Cathay Financial (2882.TW) +0.2% at NT$49.10. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0252 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.7% at 4631, hits 4628.6 as materials and financials retreat after recent gains. Index has slipped below former resistance at 4650, exposing the 200-day moving average support line at 4629.0. But it remains bullish while above former neckline resistance at 4590, from inverse head and shoulders bottom which targets 5025, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. Traders blame loss of momentum after index hit 4-month high of 4670.1 yesterday, having risen 15% in 3 weeks. "There are some downside risks after the run we've had, but I think people want to use any weakness to buy into the market," says IG Markets institutional trader Chris Weston. "I think people generally are looking for a good year end now that we've moved well into September without the usual weakness on Wall Street. They're just looking for better entry levels after a strong run in banks and materials." Expects support to emerge near 4600.0. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0236 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 0.3% at 9544.81 midday, trimming earlier gains as USD/JPY at 85.40, well off post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  peak; investors expect further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from government, says analysts. "Investors have learned what the downside threshold for USD/JPY would be, but they are trying to ascertain the upside limit," says Shinichiro Matsushita, market analyst at Daiwa Securities. Says investors see government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in event USD/JPY falls under 83, but they are still testing how high (in terms of rising USD/JPY level) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest goes. Adds Nikkei may trade between 9500-9700 rest of day. Only 14/33 Topix subindexes higher, led primarily by exporters (broader Topix off 0.1% at 848.25); Honda (7267.TO) up 0.4% at Y2,957 vs intraday high of Y2,987. \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) up 1.1% at Y2,625 vs intraday high of Y2,639. Financials generally lower; MUFG (8306.TO) flat at Y417, Dai-ichi Life (8750.TO) down 3.5% at Y102,500, \uc2 Nomura Holdings\uc2  (8604.TO) off 1.5% at Y452. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0233 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds steady given unchanged yield on 3-month PBOC bills sold in regular open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Shenyin Wanguo Securities trader notes unmoved bill yield comes as PBOC cuts sale size to CNY14 billion from CNY17 billion last week, move likely indicates \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to ease investors' concerns about possibility of interest rate hikes. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to stabilize the money market as two long public holidays approach." Mid-Autumn Festival will take place Sep. 22-24, National Day holiday Oct. 1-7. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.83, unchanged vs last close, tipped in narrow 126.8-126.9 band rest of session. (ming.wang@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 2327ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g0002a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
