\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:Indonesia Shares Tipped Up, But Profit Taking Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,466 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 04:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares tipped higher tracking gains on Wall Street, but profit taking likely looms after main index rises 3.9% to 3,357.032 yesterday. "Chart-wise, the stocks are already overbought, but sentiment on Indonesia is still positive, with the main index expected to rise to 3,500 area by year end," dealer with local securities house says. Index tipped in 3,270-3,390 range. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares down 0.1% at 448.19 vs 445.60 earlier, with investors selling after brokerage firms said banks planning to issue large volumes of new shares to raise capital in 4Q, causing price dilution. "Investor concerns are justified because inflows remain weak, but the market will soon have to absorb lots of new equity," says trader with Saigon Securities. Adds, most players staying on sidelines awaiting details of government's new financial measures to support stock market, after announcement last week it would stabilize market; otherwise more falls are expected. Tips key index in 445-450 range. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD tad lower at 31.785 vs 31.798 yesterday; local bank trader says few trades in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market so far on lack of strong leads from regional \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, local shares (Taiex last down 0.3%). Tips pair in 31.750-31.850 band for day. Notes "the downward trend of the pair is still intact for the next 1 to 2 months as Taiex is still sustaining at highs." (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may lift its two key policy rates by 25 basis points each later in bid to rein in inflationary expectations as economy continues to show robust growth. 10 of 18 economists polled by Dow Jones expect RBI to raise repo, reverse repo rate by 25 bps, while four expect 25 bps hike in only reverse repo. "The economy is on a roll, clearly, but so are price pressures. This requires a determined tap on the brake by policy-makers. Rates need to go higher still," \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist Frederic Neumann says. India's August inflation 9.5% on-year, slower than July 9.97%, below 9.6% estimated in Dow Jones poll, as per old series. Factory output for July +13.8%, well-above estimated 7.8%. Decision due 0630 GMT. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com, khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY drops to fresh low under current system started in 1994 after PBOC surprises market by another record low dollar-yuan fixing (6.7181 vs 6.7250 yesterday). On OTC, USD/CNY at 6.7298 vs 6.7422 last close; tipped 6.7250-6.7330 for session. "Beijing's attitude is obvious ahead of the key U.S. Congressional hearing on the yuan issue," says Shanghai-based trader at local bank. "China basically says it would allow the yuan to rise, just that it has to be under the background of strong economic data." Still, USD demand expected to dominate intraday, as those with CNY needs stay on sidelines in anticipation of better levels, he adds. Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY NDF at 6.6480/6.6550 vs 6.6646/6.6686 late yesterday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares down early as investors stay cautious amid uncertainty over possibility of PBOC rate hike. Shanghai Composite Index down 0.3% at 2645.03, tipped to stay in 2650-2700 range for now. "Cautious investors are scrambling back funds ahead of next Wednesday's holiday and Golden Week in case Beijing launches new policies or global markets see big moves," says Hao Guomei at Huatai Securities. Tips rate hike unlikely, but PBOC's silence on matter worrying investors. Banks down slightly, extending losses from previous session. ICBC (601398.SH) down 0.3% at CNY4.02, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (601939.SH) down 0.9% at CNY4.57. Automakers down on profit taking; SAIC (600104.SH) down 1.4% at CNY16.75, FAW Car (000800.SZ) down 1.1% at CNY18.80. Shenzhen index down 0.2% at 1179.64. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds steady in quiet trade, amid lack of decisive leads; 3-year yield flat at 3.43%, 5-year yield down 1 bp at 3.86%, September futures +7 ticks at 112.75. Bond manager at local securities firm expects bond sentiment to remain relatively positive for rest of session; notes, "foreigners started off the session by selling futures actively, but they're buying them back now," lifting sentiment. Adds, futures fell to as low as 112.55 earlier in morning, but bounced back and breached 112.70 resistance. Puts cap at 112.90. Notes, bonds fell yesterday in wake of JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities, but weaker JPY not necessarily negative cue for local bonds as weaker JPY/KRW would mean poorer price competitiveness for local exporters in longer-term, thereby bond-positive. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0141 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR steady at 8,980 vs 8,975 late yesterday in sluggish trade as market awaiting fresh cues, dealers say. "I think the dollar will trade in a narrow range of between IDR8,970-IDR8,990 today," dealer says; adds market awaiting clear USD direction vs majors after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday, its fist in more than 6 years. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0132 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares may open higher after comments from \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  governor Wednesday helped calm jitters that Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may impose more steps to stem THB's rise; such speculation had been pressuring stocks in recent sessions. Eyes on foreign investors' activity after they have been net sellers over past 2 sessions. "By staying above the 900 psychological level yesterday, the SET index has proved that the trend is still biased upward," says KT Zmico Securities' First VP Charoen Iamphattanatham. Adds players, who locked in profit in recent downward correction, may start thinking about reentering. Resistance put at 930, support at 915. SET Index closed flat at 2-week-low of 921.10 yesterday. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tipped higher, taking cue from overnight gains on Wall Street, steady inflow of foreign funds; benchmark PSEi likely to head towards 4000 resistance after closing at new record high +0.1% at 3973.48 Wednesday; support at 3950. News from ongoing Philippine mining conference likely to underpin mining firms, led by gold-copper producer Philex (PX.PH). "We'll probably see more modest gains at we near 4000," says Jose Vistan, research head at AB Capital Securities. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares continue to inch up, driving STI closer to 3100, which expected to offer resistance. Index +0.3% at 3081.23, just off new 2010 high of 3086.28 set in early trade. But having gained as much as 2.1% so far this week, selling on strength possible any time. "Support at 3040 is strong, and this level has been tested thrice since April. This level must hold steady in order for the bulls to push on higher," says \uc2 Kim Eng Securities\uc2 . Notable gainers among STI components include Genting Singapore (G13.SG) +2.0% at S$2.01, \uc2 Jardine Strategic\uc2  (J37.SG) +1.1% at US$26.66, \uc2 Jardine Matheson\uc2  (J36.SG) +1.0% at US$44.52, \uc2 SingTel\uc2  (Z74.SG) +1.3% at S$3.11. Market breadth at 2 gainers for each decliner. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] If Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (BOJ) recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  works to further weaken yen, this could be positive for Japan's stock market as correlation between USD/JPY and Topix remains fairly high, says Kathy Matsui, chief Japan strategist at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . Adds, while Finance Minister Noda said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was done unilaterally, and another MOF official said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "is not finished," GS Global Markets team says it is conceivable Japanese government will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in response to similar volatile scenarios. But adds, brokerage does not rule out possibility of new low for USD/JPY as 1) it is unlikely Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be endorsed by other governments, 2) GS expects U.S. Federal Reserve will embark on another round of large-scale quantitative easing later this year, which could lead to lower U.S. rates and weaker USD. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 2208ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g0001i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
