\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: RBI May Hike Repo, Reverse Repo 25BPs Each -Poll\b0\par\par\uc2 1,535 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 04:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may lift its two key policy rates by 25 basis points each later in bid to rein in inflationary expectations as economy continues to show robust growth. 10 of 18 economists polled by Dow Jones expect RBI to raise repo, reverse repo rate by 25 bps, while four expect 25 bps hike in only reverse repo. "The economy is on a roll, clearly, but so are price pressures. This requires a determined tap on the brake by policy-makers. Rates need to go higher still," \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist Frederic Neumann says. India's August inflation 9.5% on-year, slower than July 9.97%, below 9.6% estimated in Dow Jones poll, as per old series. Factory output for July +13.8%, well-above estimated 7.8%. Decision due 0630 GMT. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com, khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY drops to fresh low under current system started in 1994 after PBOC surprises market by another record low dollar-yuan fixing (6.7181 vs 6.7250 yesterday). On OTC, USD/CNY at 6.7298 vs 6.7422 last close; tipped 6.7250-6.7330 for session. "Beijing's attitude is obvious ahead of the key U.S. Congressional hearing on the yuan issue," says Shanghai-based trader at local bank. "China basically says it would allow the yuan to rise, just that it has to be under the background of strong economic data." Still, USD demand expected to dominate intraday, as those with CNY needs stay on sidelines in anticipation of better levels, he adds. Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY NDF at 6.6480/6.6550 vs 6.6646/6.6686 late yesterday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares down early as investors stay cautious amid uncertainty over possibility of PBOC rate hike. Shanghai Composite Index down 0.3% at 2645.03, tipped to stay in 2650-2700 range for now. "Cautious investors are scrambling back funds ahead of next Wednesday's holiday and Golden Week in case Beijing launches new policies or global markets see big moves," says Hao Guomei at Huatai Securities. Tips rate hike unlikely, but PBOC's silence on matter worrying investors. Banks down slightly, extending losses from previous session. ICBC (601398.SH) down 0.3% at CNY4.02, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (601939.SH) down 0.9% at CNY4.57. Automakers down on profit taking; SAIC (600104.SH) down 1.4% at CNY16.75, FAW Car (000800.SZ) down 1.1% at CNY18.80. Shenzhen index down 0.2% at 1179.64. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds steady in quiet trade, amid lack of decisive leads; 3-year yield flat at 3.43%, 5-year yield down 1 bp at 3.86%, September futures +7 ticks at 112.75. Bond manager at local securities firm expects bond sentiment to remain relatively positive for rest of session; notes, "foreigners started off the session by selling futures actively, but they're buying them back now," lifting sentiment. Adds, futures fell to as low as 112.55 earlier in morning, but bounced back and breached 112.70 resistance. Puts cap at 112.90. Notes, bonds fell yesterday in wake of JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities, but weaker JPY not necessarily negative cue for local bonds as weaker JPY/KRW would mean poorer price competitiveness for local exporters in longer-term, thereby bond-positive. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0141 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR steady at 8,980 vs 8,975 late yesterday in sluggish trade as market awaiting fresh cues, dealers say.