\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: China Shares Down On Caution Ahead of Holidays\b0\par\par\uc2 1,634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 03:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares down early as investors stay cautious amid uncertainty over possibility of PBOC rate hike. Shanghai Composite Index down 0.3% at 2645.03, tipped to stay in 2650-2700 range for now. "Cautious investors are scrambling back funds ahead of next Wednesday's holiday and Golden Week in case Beijing launches new policies or global markets see big moves," says Hao Guomei at Huatai Securities. Tips rate hike unlikely, but PBOC's silence on matter worrying investors. Banks down slightly, extending losses from previous session. ICBC (601398.SH) down 0.3% at CNY4.02, \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Construction Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  (601939.SH) down 0.9% at CNY4.57. Automakers down on profit taking; SAIC (600104.SH) down 1.4% at CNY16.75, FAW Car (000800.SZ) down 1.1% at CNY18.80. Shenzhen index down 0.2% at 1179.64. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds steady in quiet trade, amid lack of decisive leads; 3-year yield flat at 3.43%, 5-year yield down 1 bp at 3.86%, September futures +7 ticks at 112.75. Bond manager at local securities firm expects bond sentiment to remain relatively positive for rest of session; notes, "foreigners started off the session by selling futures actively, but they're buying them back now," lifting sentiment. Adds, futures fell to as low as 112.55 earlier in morning, but bounced back and breached 112.70 resistance. Puts cap at 112.90. Notes, bonds fell yesterday in wake of JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities, but weaker JPY not necessarily negative cue for local bonds as weaker JPY/KRW would mean poorer price competitiveness for local exporters in longer-term, thereby bond-positive. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0141 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR steady at 8,980 vs 8,975 late yesterday in sluggish trade as market awaiting fresh cues, dealers say. "I think the dollar will trade in a narrow range of between IDR8,970-IDR8,990 today," dealer says; adds market awaiting clear USD direction vs majors after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday, its fist in more than 6 years. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0132 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares may open higher after comments from \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  governor Wednesday helped calm jitters that Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may impose more steps to stem THB's rise; such speculation had been pressuring stocks in recent sessions. Eyes on foreign investors' activity after they have been net sellers over past 2 sessions. "By staying above the 900 psychological level yesterday, the SET index has proved that the trend is still biased upward," says KT Zmico Securities' First VP Charoen Iamphattanatham. Adds players, who locked in profit in recent downward correction, may start thinking about reentering. Resistance put at 930, support at 915. SET Index closed flat at 2-week-low of 921.10 yesterday. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tipped higher, taking cue from overnight gains on Wall Street, steady inflow of foreign funds; benchmark PSEi likely to head towards 4000 resistance after closing at new record high +0.1% at 3973.48 Wednesday; support at 3950. News from ongoing Philippine mining conference likely to underpin mining firms, led by gold-copper producer Philex (PX.PH). "We'll probably see more modest gains at we near 4000," says Jose Vistan, research head at AB Capital Securities. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares continue to inch up, driving STI closer to 3100, which expected to offer resistance. Index +0.3% at 3081.23, just off new 2010 high of 3086.28 set in early trade. But having gained as much as 2.1% so far this week, selling on strength possible any time. "Support at 3040 is strong, and this level has been tested thrice since April. This level must hold steady in order for the bulls to push on higher," says \uc2 Kim Eng Securities\uc2 . Notable gainers among STI components include Genting Singapore (G13.SG) +2.0% at S$2.01, \uc2 Jardine Strategic\uc2  (J37.SG) +1.1% at US$26.66, \uc2 Jardine Matheson\uc2  (J36.SG) +1.0% at US$44.52, \uc2 SingTel\uc2  (Z74.SG) +1.3% at S$3.11. Market breadth at 2 gainers for each decliner. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] If Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (BOJ) recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  works to further weaken yen, this could be positive for Japan's stock market as correlation between USD/JPY and Topix remains fairly high, says Kathy Matsui, chief Japan strategist at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . Adds, while Finance Minister Noda said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was done unilaterally, and another MOF official said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "is not finished," GS Global Markets team says it is conceivable Japanese government will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in response to similar volatile scenarios. But adds, brokerage does not rule out possibility of new low for USD/JPY as 1) it is unlikely Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be endorsed by other governments, 2) GS expects U.S. Federal Reserve will embark on another round of large-scale quantitative easing later this year, which could lead to lower U.S. rates and weaker USD. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0053 GMT misstated the time of BOJ's second money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The correct version follows:) BOJ skips 0030 GMT market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , refraining--as expected--from draining any funds from short-term market, in sign it's not yet sterilizing \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maximize effects of government effort to weaken JPY; market now eying another BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 0400 GMT. Excluding yesterday's huge \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , regular funding demands (fiscal payments etc. unrelated to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) would require BOJ to inject Y700 billion; market participants suspect BOJ may leave JPY, from \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in market, go about its regular business, meaning may inject Y700 billion into market. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0118 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may stay in range after rising for 5th straight session, with Ben Kwong at KGI tipping index around 21,700-21,800 range today. "I expect the index to stay in a narrow trading band today due to an absence of fresh catalysts." But adds overall trading sentiment remains positive, and expects further buying interest in run-up to end-September due to window-dressing. Gold stocks may continue to outperform given gold price's recent record-breaking run, while oil stocks may see some weakness after softer crude prices on Nymex overnight. HSI ends +0.1% at 21,725.64 yesterday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0117 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian companies are about to enter into an investment/M&A cycle because balance sheets are lowly geared and asset values are off their peaks, says RBS. Identifies more than A$45 billion worth of stocks that are potentially "in play", with candidates mainly in utilities, consumer discretionary, consumer staples, energy and industrials/agriculture sectors. Argues that M&A is more likely to occur in sectors delivering product that the world is structurally short, particularly food and energy. S&P/ASX 200 last down 0.2% at 4651.3. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0117 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0004 GMT misstated the time of BOJ's money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The correct version follows:) Market focused on BOJ money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  (at 0030 GMT, 0400 GMT) to determine if it's sterilizing MOF's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; sources have told Dow Jones BOJ to leave extra yen from MOF's selling-spree yesterday in market, so MOF's effort would pack more punch. BOJ current account balance (Y15.19 trillion as of Wednesday) would soar to around Y17 trillion Friday (2-day settlement) if BOJ leaves the Y2 trillion MOF estimated to have sold in Wednesday's massive campaign to weaken yen. BOJ likely to skip 0030 GMT \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but if it drains any funds from market that'd indicate it's sterilizing some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; at 0400 GMT, absent yesterday's huge \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , regular funding demands (fiscal payments etc) would require BOJ to inject Y700 billion. Market players suspect BOJ may leave yen from \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in market, go about its regular business, meaning it may inject Y700 billion into market at 0400 GMT. Again, if BOJ drains any funds, that'd be clear sign it's sterilizing, in what would surely draw strong, if veiled criticism from government, which wants BOJ to cooperate in easing monetary conditions to fight strong yen, deflation. (hiroshi.miwa@dowjones.com; arran.scott@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0115 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may consolidate with upward bias on lingering concerns over likelihood of PBOC interest rate hike. Shanghai Composite Index tipped to trade in 2650-2750 range, after ending down 1.3% at 2652.50 yesterday. "Investors are still uncertain about the possibility of a PBOC rate hike. With the long holidays ahead, market sentiment is extra cautious," says Wei Daoke, analyst at Shenyin Wanguo Securities. Nation to go on 3-day Mid-Autumn Festival holiday starting Sept. 22 and 7-day Golden Week holiday from Oct. 1. PBOC so far has declined to comment on rate hike rumors, but NDRC said late Wednesday that China can "absolutely" meet its target of around a 3.0% rise in CPI this year, despite the index rising 3.5% on-year in August. Cement, steel sectors may rise after Wen Jiabao pledged to step up flood prevention measures Wednesday. Shenzhen index down 2.1% at 1182.23. (jean.yung@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 2151ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g0001d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
