\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Good, But New USD/JPY Low Possible-GS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,563 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 03:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] If Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's (BOJ) recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  works to further weaken yen, this could be positive for Japan's stock market as correlation between USD/JPY and Topix remains fairly high, says Kathy Matsui, chief Japan strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Adds, while Finance Minister Noda said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was done unilaterally, and another MOF official said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "is not finished," GS Global Markets team says it is conceivable Japanese government will continue to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in response to similar volatile scenarios. But adds, brokerage does not rule out possibility of new low for USD/JPY as 1) it is unlikely Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be endorsed by other governments, 2) GS expects U.S. Federal Reserve will embark on another round of large-scale quantitative easing later this year, which could lead to lower U.S. rates and weaker USD. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0053 GMT misstated the time of BOJ's second money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The correct version follows:) BOJ skips 0030 GMT market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , refraining--as expected--from draining any funds from short-term market, in sign it's not yet sterilizing \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maximize effects of government effort to weaken JPY; market now eying another BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 0400 GMT. Excluding yesterday's huge \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , regular funding demands (fiscal payments etc. unrelated to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) would require BOJ to inject Y700 billion; market participants suspect BOJ may leave JPY, from \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in market, go about its regular business, meaning may inject Y700 billion into market. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0118 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may stay in range after rising for 5th straight session, with Ben Kwong at KGI tipping index around 21,700-21,800 range today. "I expect the index to stay in a narrow trading band today due to an absence of fresh catalysts." But adds overall trading sentiment remains positive, and expects further buying interest in run-up to end-September due to window-dressing. Gold stocks may continue to outperform given gold price's recent record-breaking run, while oil stocks may see some weakness after softer crude prices on Nymex overnight. HSI ends +0.1% at 21,725.64 yesterday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0117 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian companies are about to enter into an investment/M&A cycle because balance sheets are lowly geared and asset values are off their peaks, says RBS. Identifies more than A$45 billion worth of stocks that are potentially "in play", with candidates mainly in utilities, consumer discretionary, consumer staples, energy and industrials/agriculture sectors. Argues that M&A is more likely to occur in sectors delivering product that the world is structurally short, particularly food and energy. S&P/ASX 200 last down 0.2% at 4651.3. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0117 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0004 GMT misstated the time of BOJ's money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The correct version follows:) Market focused on BOJ money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  (at 0030 GMT, 0400 GMT) to determine if it's sterilizing MOF's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; sources have told Dow Jones BOJ to leave extra yen from MOF's selling-spree yesterday in market, so MOF's effort would pack more punch. BOJ current account balance (Y15.19 trillion as of Wednesday) would soar to around Y17 trillion Friday (2-day settlement) if BOJ leaves the Y2 trillion MOF estimated to have sold in Wednesday's massive campaign to weaken yen. BOJ likely to skip 0030 GMT \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but if it drains any funds from market that'd indicate it's sterilizing some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; at 0400 GMT, absent yesterday's huge \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , regular funding demands (fiscal payments etc) would require BOJ to inject Y700 billion. Market players suspect BOJ may leave yen from \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in market, go about its regular business, meaning it may inject Y700 billion into market at 0400 GMT. Again, if BOJ drains any funds, that'd be clear sign it's sterilizing, in what would surely draw strong, if veiled criticism from government, which wants BOJ to cooperate in easing monetary conditions to fight strong yen, deflation. (hiroshi.miwa@dowjones.com; arran.scott@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0115 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may consolidate with upward bias on lingering concerns over likelihood of PBOC interest rate hike. Shanghai Composite Index tipped to trade in 2650-2750 range, after ending down 1.3% at 2652.50 yesterday. "Investors are still uncertain about the possibility of a PBOC rate hike. With the long holidays ahead, market sentiment is extra cautious," says Wei Daoke, analyst at Shenyin Wanguo Securities. Nation to go on 3-day Mid-Autumn Festival holiday starting Sept. 22 and 7-day Golden Week holiday from Oct. 1. PBOC so far has declined to comment on rate hike rumors, but NDRC said late Wednesday that China can "absolutely" meet its target of around a 3.0% rise in CPI this year, despite the index rising 3.5% on-year in August. Cement, steel sectors may rise after Wen Jiabao pledged to step up flood prevention measures Wednesday. Shenzhen index down 2.1% at 1182.23. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0113 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD higher on stronger USD offshore, says senior trader at U.S. bank. Tips pair in 7.7650-7.7690 band near term. "I expect the pair to stay in a tight range...the recent strength of the yuan may continue to lend support to the Hong Kong dollar as the local unit often trades as a proxy for the yuan." Pair last at 7.7675 vs 7.7666 yesterday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0102 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW marginally higher at 1,162.3 vs 1,160.9 late yesterday in Seoul amid Kospi's 0.3% fall. Traders say expectations of possible USD buying by companies related to recent M&A deals keeping pair well-supported at 1,160, while market remains wary over possible USD-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Local bank trader tips pair in 1,159-1,165 range rest of day. "With the pair's downside continually stalled (at 1,160), some are even opting for going long on the dollar," near that level, he says. But notes, "dollar selling by exporters ahead of the national holiday next week will likely be at its peak today and tomorrow," which may cap pair's upside. Offshore players not providing much momentum for KRW so far today. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0100 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB steady at 30.84-30.86 vs 30.85 late yesterday in quiet trade. Players sidelined, waiting to see whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF will carry out more JPY-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, while skepticism persists after comment from BOT governor that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no immediate plan to impose more measures to stem baht rise, says local dealer. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment comes after talk in market since early this week that it may impose some measures to a curb strong baht. Pair tipped in 30.80-30.90 band. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0100 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped to fall slightly amid healthy risk appetite following overnight gains on Wall Street, steady growth in overseas Filipino workers' remittance (+8.2% on year in July), traders say; however, weaker EUR in early Asian trade, possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in JPY may provide support for USD/PHP, they say; pair may move in 44.15-44.35 range early after settling unchanged at 44.26 late Wednesday; wider trading band still pegged at 44.00-44.50. "Sentiment is still bullish for the peso. I still think we'll end the year at the 43 level (vs USD)," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0059 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY could rise after 4 consecutive sessions of sharp fall, following rebound of dollar index overnight; but uncertainties remain on how PBOC to set fixing, eyed likely tad above record low of 6.7250 yesterday. "If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sets the fixing 100-150 points higher than yesterday, that would indicate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  intends to have the yuan trade in a relatively large band in the near term," says Shanghai-based trader at Asian bank. "If it's near the level yesterday, or unchanged, then that would indicate PBOC may be allowing a yuan appreciation trend." Traders to monitor closely result of U.S. Congressional hearings on yuan policy, but so far no strong market reaction anticipated, he adds. USD/CNY tipped 6.7200-6.7400 for session. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0055 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to climb higher for 5th session, with financial stocks probably outperforming as local regulator says whole banking sector passes stress test, says KGI Securities analyst Randy Chang; "demand (for stocks) will likely come from local institutional investors and government funds." Tips Taiex to test immediate resistance at 8200, turnover likely to continue to exceed NT$140 billion. Taiex yesterday ends +0.4% at 8163.82, turnover at high NT$144.49 billion. (aries.poon@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 2126ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g00017\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
