ex overnight; but uncertainties remain on how PBOC to set fixing, eyed likely tad above record low of 6.7250 yesterday. "If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sets the fixing 100-150 points higher than yesterday, that would indicate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  intends to have the yuan trade in a relatively large band in the near term," says Shanghai-based trader at Asian bank. "If it's near the level yesterday, or unchanged, then that would indicate PBOC may be allowing a yuan appreciation trend." Traders to monitor closely result of U.S. Congressional hearings on yuan policy, but so far no strong market reaction anticipated, he adds. USD/CNY tipped 6.7200-6.7400 for session. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0055 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to climb higher for 5th session, with financial stocks probably outperforming as local regulator says whole banking sector passes stress test, says KGI Securities analyst Randy Chang; "demand (for stocks) will likely come from local institutional investors and government funds." Tips Taiex to test immediate resistance at 8200, turnover likely to continue to exceed NT$140 billion. Taiex yesterday ends +0.4% at 8163.82, turnover at high NT$144.49 billion. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0053 GMT [Dow Jones] BOJ skips 0030 GMT market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , refraining--as expected--from draining any funds from short-term market, in sign it's not yet sterilizing \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maximize effects of government effort to weaken JPY; market now eying another BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 0430 GMT. Excluding yesterday's huge \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , regular funding demands (fiscal payments etc. unrelated to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) would require BOJ to inject Y700 billion; market participants suspect BOJ may leave JPY, from \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in market, go about its regular business, meaning may inject Y700 billion into market. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0052 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY falls to 85.43 as Japan exporters sell at levels they consider attractive, says senior dealer at major European bank. Pair may fall to 85.10 vs last 85.55; EUR/JPY falls as well to 111.02 vs last 111.15. Adds whether or not \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is markets' sole focus today. Senior trader at major \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  bank anticipates further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities if pair sustains current decline, falls further; meanwhile, another senior trader at major European bank says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  today as well, "in order to make the impact of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strong." (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0051 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely to fall in 31.680-31.780 band vs 31.798 late yesterday, weighed by exporters' selling around 31.780, expected gains in Taiwan shares, says local bank trader. Adds, unlike \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surprising \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  yesterday, Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to keep buying USD mildly on daily basis, as "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to keep the pair dynamically stable," he says. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.2% at 4652.7 after September SPI 200 futures expiry this morning, which caused the usual spike in trading volume. Few standouts in major stocks, with BHP (BHP.AU) the biggest drag on the market, down 0.7%, and \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  (NAB.AU) the main positive influence, up 1.0% after recent decision to drop its bid for AXA APH (AXA.AU). Other movers include Westfield (WDC.AU), down 1.0% and Foster's (FGL.AU) up 1.0%. Traders now expect the market to track sideways this morning, while waiting to see if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen again. "SPI expiry dominated the early activity," says Shaw Stockbroking's head of trading Jamie Spiteri. "A lot of the volume for the day will have been satisfied within the match." Says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on JPY could help trigger further short covering on Australian banks. "A lot of hedge funds have been apprehensive about our banks and financials generally, so continued rises there are putting pressure on exposed short positions." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi down 0.1% at 1821.51 on profit taking after ending at year-to-date high yesterday, analysts say; but continued foreign inflows into Korean shares likely to cushion downward pressure, may even help index reverse trend later in session. "The Kospi a bit tired, its upward strength has somewhat weakened after recent rises and ahead of the Korean holiday (September 21-23)," says \uc2 Tong Yang Securities\uc2 ' analyst Lee Jae-mahn. Lee adds JPY-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  yesterday may weigh on some exporters, but impact likely short-lived "as such government action is hardly likely to reverse any broader market trend." Market leader \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) down 0.3% at KRW755,000, pressuring broader index, while KB Financial (105560.SE) also down 1.2% at KRW49,800 after recent rises. But GS Engineering (006360.SE) +1.9% at KRW93,700 after news it wins $628.4 million orders for two Oman projects. Kospi tipped in 1810-1835 range. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0039 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  revises model portfolio for Thai stocks, but maintains Overweight rating. Initiates coverage on hospital operators with Neutral rating on \uc2 Bumrungrad Hospital\uc2  (BH.TH), Overweight rating on Bangkok Dusit Medical Services (BGH.Th). "Also, we add to existing Overweight positions in \uc2 Advanced Info Service\uc2  (ADVANC.TH) and Banpu (BANPU.TH). AIS still seems to under-price the benefits of likely 3G licensing, in our view, and we expect Banpu to perform better as the market learns more about the benefits of the Australia acquisition." Cuts Land & Houses (LH.TH) to Underweight from Neutral. Reduces Overweight positions in CP All (CPALL.TH), Central Pattana (CPN.TH) on less attractive valuations. House's model portfolio has outperformed MSCI Thailand index by 2.1% in past month, giving 0.5% outperformance year to date. Portfolio has outperformed MSCI Asia ex Japan index by 5.2% in past month, by 27.5% year to date. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0032 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD nearly flat at 1.3374 vs 1.3371 late Asia trade yesterday as some investors remain on sidelines on expectations that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MOF might carry out more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves to defend USD, says trader with local bank. "Risk appetite is positive, and the market is short USD/Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but investors won't push the USD to new lows on worries of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (from Asian central banks)," trader says. Tips pair to trade in 1.3350-1.3400 range. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0030 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 0.7% at 9585.52 with exporter buying continuing from Wednesday as USD/JPY trading above 85 (now 85.43) post-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; Nikkei's resistance seen at 9750, say market analysts. "Foreign investors' appetite in Japan stocks is also lifting sentiment," says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets, adding 5 consecutive days of premarket net buying and relatively high net buy figures by foreign investors suggest high total trading volume today. 28/33 Topix subsectors in positive territory; \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) up 2.3% at Y3,080, \uc2 Toshiba\uc2  (6502.TO) up 2.0% at Y414, \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) up 1.3% at Y2,982. Inpex (1605.TO) down 0.6% at Y409,000 on falling crude price and \uc2 UBS\uc2  rating cut to Neutral. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 2100ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g0000r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
