\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Falls As Japan Exporters Sell\b0\par\par\uc2 1,703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 02:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0052 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY falls to 85.43 as Japan exporters sell at levels they consider attractive, says senior dealer at major European bank. Pair may fall to 85.10 vs last 85.55; EUR/JPY falls as well to 111.02 vs last 111.15. Adds whether or not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities continue to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market is markets' sole focus today. Senior trader at major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bank anticipates further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities if pair sustains current decline, falls further; meanwhile, another senior trader at major European bank says \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities should \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  today as well, "in order to make the impact of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strong." (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0051 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely to fall in 31.680-31.780 band vs 31.798 late yesterday, weighed by exporters' selling around 31.780, expected gains in Taiwan shares, says local bank trader. Adds, unlike \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surprising \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  yesterday, Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to keep buying USD mildly on daily basis, as "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to keep the pair dynamically stable," he says. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.2% at 4652.7 after September SPI 200 futures expiry this morning, which caused the usual spike in trading volume. Few standouts in major stocks, with BHP (BHP.AU) the biggest drag on the market, down 0.7%, and \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  (NAB.AU) the main positive influence, up 1.0% after recent decision to drop its bid for AXA APH (AXA.AU). Other movers include Westfield (WDC.AU), down 1.0% and Foster's (FGL.AU) up 1.0%. Traders now expect the market to track sideways this morning, while waiting to see if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen again. "SPI expiry dominated the early activity," says Shaw Stockbroking's head of trading Jamie Spiteri. "A lot of the volume for the day will have been satisfied within the match." Says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on JPY could help trigger further short covering on Australian banks. "A lot of hedge funds have been apprehensive about our banks and financials generally, so continued rises there are putting pressure on exposed short positions." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi down 0.1% at 1821.51 on profit taking after ending at year-to-date high yesterday, analysts say; but continued foreign inflows into Korean shares likely to cushion downward pressure, may even help index reverse trend later in session. "The Kospi a bit tired, its upward strength has somewhat weakened after recent rises and ahead of the Korean holiday (September 21-23)," says \uc2 Tong Yang Securities\uc2 ' analyst Lee Jae-mahn. Lee adds JPY-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  yesterday may weigh on some exporters, but impact likely short-lived "as such government action is hardly likely to reverse any broader market trend." Market leader \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) down 0.3% at KRW755,000, pressuring broader index, while KB Financial (105560.SE) also down 1.2% at KRW49,800 after recent rises. But GS Engineering (006360.SE) +1.9% at KRW93,700 after news it wins $628.4 million orders for two Oman projects. Kospi tipped in 1810-1835 range. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0039 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  revises model portfolio for Thai stocks, but maintains Overweight rating. Initiates coverage on hospital operators with Neutral rating on \uc2 Bumrungrad Hospital\uc2  (BH.TH), Overweight rating on Bangkok Dusit Medical Services (BGH.Th). "Also, we add to existing Overweight positions in \uc2 Advanced Info Service\uc2  (ADVANC.TH) and Banpu (BANPU.TH). AIS still seems to under-price the benefits of likely 3G licensing, in our view, and we expect Banpu to perform better as the market learns more about the benefits of the Australia acquisition." Cuts Land & Houses (LH.TH) to Underweight from Neutral. Reduces Overweight positions in CP All (CPALL.TH), Central Pattana (CPN.TH) on less attractive valuations. House's model portfolio has outperformed MSCI Thailand index by 2.1% in past month, giving 0.5% outperformance year to date. Portfolio has outperformed MSCI Asia ex Japan index by 5.2% in past month, by 27.5% year to date. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0032 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD nearly flat at 1.3374 vs 1.3371 late Asia trade yesterday as some investors remain on sidelines on expectations that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MOF might carry out more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves to defend USD, says trader with local bank. "Risk appetite is positive, and the market is short USD/Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but investors won't push the USD to new lows on worries of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (from Asian central banks)," trader says. Tips pair to trade in 1.3350-1.3400 range. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0030 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 0.7% at 9585.52 with exporter buying continuing from Wednesday as USD/JPY trading above 85 (now 85.43) post-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; Nikkei's resistance seen at 9750, say market analysts. "Foreign investors' appetite in Japan stocks is also lifting sentiment," says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets, adding 5 consecutive days of premarket net buying and relatively high net buy figures by foreign investors suggest high total trading volume today. 28/33 Topix subsectors in positive territory; \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) up 2.3% at Y3,080, \uc2 Toshiba\uc2  (6502.TO) up 2.0% at Y414, \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) up 1.3% at Y2,982. Inpex (1605.TO) down 0.6% at Y409,000 on falling crude price and \uc2 UBS\uc2  rating cut to Neutral. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0026 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore private home sales likely to slow in short term following recent housing-market curbs, with slower immigration resulting in reduced primary sales volumes in medium to long term, says \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 . Noting Singapore government's aim to consolidate pace of immigration inflow, house expects primary home sales to pull back to 7,000 units annually from average of 10,773 over 2005-2009; "recent cooling measures have so far had a very modest impact, but we expect this impact to increase or the government to take further measures." Says outlook for commercial properties better, with strong bids for retail, hotel sites sold under recent government land tenders boding well for higher capital values. Remains cautious on stocks of developers like CityDev (C09.SG), prefers REITs, with top pick being Ascendas REIT (A17U.SG). (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0020 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds likely in mixed trade, tracking overnight moves in U.S. Treasurys with some demand seen in short-end, while longer-dated securities might see selling, says trader with local bank. "Yesterday's surprise \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  saw some investors getting out from bonds to move into equities and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Some expect more such moves from Japan," trader says; adds, moves in local bonds won't be big given that some investors still worried that global economic recovery story still not firm. Tips 2-year bond yield to fall between 1-2 bps, while 10-year may gain 3-4 bps. Yesterday, 2-year bond yield ended 1 bps higher at 0.50%, while 10-year rose 6 bps to finish 2.19%. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0016 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW mostly steady at 1,161.2 vs 1,160.9 late yesterday in Seoul amid a flat Kospi, while traders expect tussle around 1,160 between exporter selling and possible USD buying by companies related to recent M&A deals. Sentiment remains favorable for a weaker pair following gains on Wall Street, anticipated exporter sales ahead of national holiday next week, "but the pair's fall has been continually stalled at 1,160 so far, which will make players reluctant about taking any aggressive short-dollar positions near there," says local bank trader. Tips pair in 1,156-1,165 range. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0013 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says Japan's equity markets could become volatile amid declining expectations for political change following Prime Minister Kan's Democratic Party of Japan (DPJ) election victory. Also notes stocks face mounting selling pressure toward deadline for squaring long positions in margin accounts during September. Despite government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, adds increasing repatriation of funds could buttress yen this month. "Conversely though, selling pressure should ease on the equity market by the end of the month. All in all, it should take some time before negative catalysts are priced in." (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0009 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals face divergent short-term outlooks, says Triland Metals citing copper, aluminum as examples; notes Europe's biggest copper producer, Aurubis (NDE.XE) said European demand rising, with further increase in demand for new copper expected from China. Chinese manufacturers expected to intensify copper purchases after quiet summer, and while metals still rangebound, copper could move higher from fundamental perspective. As for aluminum, house says U.S. industrial production lower than estimates, however not devastatingly so. That said, appears \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  largely supporting aluminum at this level, so pull back in price shouldn't be ruled out, indeed, a correction lower could be around corner, though not a big one as there's plenty to cheer about on relative basis, Triland says in daily note. Meanwhile, major market participant that requests anonymity, says while prices settled mixed overnight, they "consolidated;" "Base metals prices actually held fairly well" amid issue of weak U.S. industrial output data, talk of more policy tightening in China which caused further concerns over upcoming demand, it says in morning note. LME 3-month copper fell $35 vs Tuesday PM kerb to $7,615/metric ton, aluminum eased $7 at $2,153, zinc shed $28 at $2,145.00, nickel slipped $150 to $23,200, lead lost $7 to $2,235, but tin extended rally, surging $505 to $23,150, amid concerns about Indonesian supply. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 2052ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100916e69g0000l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal