\par\par \b \uc2 HEARD ON THE STREET: Tokyo's Yen Shift Poses Trader Puzzle\b0\par\par\uc2 By Richard Barley and Simon Nixon   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES COLUMN   \par \uc2 503 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 17:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 How should investors respond to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surprise \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the strength of the yen? That depends largely on whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  turns out to be a political gesture, limited to the roughly Y2 trillion so far spent; or something more like the 2003-2004 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the tune of Y35 trillion. Western policymakers, believing the exercise to be misguided and potentially damaging, will be hoping for the former; but Tokyo certain has room in its budget plans to fund a bigger program.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the initial impact on exchange rates other than dollar-yen has been limited. While the yen has fallen 3.3% against the dollar, the dollar hasn't gained against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But if the yen is no longer a one-way trade, investors could switch to commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Australian dollar, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . If dollar weakness causes emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gain, the risk is that central banks elsewhere in Asia and Latin America also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo's move is also likely to affect fixed-income markets, particularly as the Bank of Japan seems intent on leaving the funds in the market, making this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  a form of quantitative easing as well. Japanese government bond yields fell Wednesday and could fall further if more liquidity floods into the market. But it will be crucial to watch Japanese fiscal policy, since further quantitative easing could increase sovereign credit risk. Japanese sovereign credit-default swaps have so far remained flat at 67 basis points.\par \par \uc2 Of course, it's possible a lower yen will succeed in boosting Japanese exporters, which over time will be reflected in investors switching out of bonds into equities. But exports have never been Japan's problem: the real issue is the lack of domestic demand. And on that score, Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , by reducing the value of domestic earnings, may be self-defeating. That would point to Japan's actions being a political gesture, albeit a futile one. Tokyo's rhetoric suggests otherwise.\par \par \uc2 (Simon Nixon is European editor of Heard on the Street. He can be contacted on +44 207 842 9206 and simon.nixon@wsj.com. Richard Barley is a writer for Heard on the Street. He has covered the European corporate credit market in one form or another since 1998. He can be reached at +44-20-7842-9406 or by email: richard.barley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 16-09-10 1524GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5090   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100916e69g0014x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
