\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Yen Bounces A Little, Investors Wait For Geithner\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 11:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The yen bounced back a little Thursday as the Bank of Japan stayed out of the market and investors wait to see how U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner reacts to Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 See how the dollar has eased back a little against the yen:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4602"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4602}}}\par \par \uc2 So far, the U.S. Treasury has declined to comment officially on what now appears to have been the largest ever \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities, where about Y2 trillion was spent pushing the dollar back up to about Y85.50 from just under Y83.00.\par \par \uc2 However, fears that Geithner might be critical of the move have risen after the chairman of the U.S. House Ways and Means Committee, Sander Levin, described the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as "deeply disturbing."\par \par \uc2 Geithner is now expected to be questioned on Japan's exercise when he appears before the committee later in the day to discuss China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 Simon Derrick, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York Mellon in London, said he expects Geithner to avoid any direct criticism of Japan. But, he added, "it seems reasonable to suppose that he may repeat the comments he made on TV a week ago, indicating that it would be better served looking for ways to reinforce the recovery."\par \par \uc2 Analysts have suggested that external response to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be important in determining whether the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  returns to the market with even more yen sales.\par \par \uc2 In other words, as long as the U.S. and euro zone don't publicly criticize the move, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if it needs to.\par \par \uc2 So far, reaction from euro zone officials has also been fairly subdued with Eurogroup Chairman Jean-Claude Juncker suggesting that coordinated rather than unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is generally more successful in correcting global imbalances.\par \par \uc2 Although fallout from the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains the primary focus, investors are also looking closely to see what the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  says when it completes its latest policy meeting later in the day.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely expected to leave rates unchanged. However, there is concern that it will also express fears about inflationary pressures and hint at the need to hike rates again soon.\par \par \uc2 If so, this could well propel the Swiss franc into another rally against the euro, pushing the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back down under CHF1.30.\par \par \uc2 The pound found itself in the firing line briefly after U.K. retail sales for August fell by a disappointing 0.5%. The market had been looking for sales to rise by 0.3%, slowing down from the 1.1% increase in July.\par \par \uc2 By midmorning, the pound was down at $1.5590 from $1.5623 late Wednesday in New York, according to EBS.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was down at Y85.56 from Y85.63 but the euro rose to Y111.76 from Y111.36.\par \par \uc2 The euro was also up at $1.3058 from $1.3012 while the dollar fell to CHF1.0017 from CHF1.0035.\par \par \uc2 -By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9493; nick.hastings@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 16-09-10 0921GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100916e69g000qe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
