\par\par \b \uc2 UK MARKET TALK ROUNDUP: BROKERS COMMENTS\b0\par\par\uc2 2,595 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 09:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Broker comments in the UK today. Compiled by Dow Jones Newswires Markets Desk, markets.eu@dowjones.com \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0705 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bluebay Asset Management\f28 \uc2 \f2 's (BBAY.LN) final results for the year to June are in line with expectations says \f28 \uc2 \f2 KBC Peel Hunt\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The dividend increase is in line with the brokerage's forecast and at the top of the range. The outlook statement is encouraging and the year has started well. KBC notes flows of $850M in the first two months and the company expects $5B-8B for the FY, comfortably ahead of $4B KBC is expecting. Buy rating and 440p target. Shares +6.1% at 340p. (andrea.tryphonides@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0655 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  upgrades \uc2 Eurasian Natural Resources\uc2  (ENRC.LN) to outperform from neutral, leaving the target at 1200p. Says ENRC has been a major underperformer since its April peak, driven by falling ferrochrome prices and concerns over M&A. Thinks bad news is in the price and the shares are being priced for little improvement in ferrochrome prices or growth upside over the medium term. Says it is time to focus on growth upside, direction of the stainless/ferrochrome cycle and ENRC's asset quality and valuation. Shares closed on Wednesday at 866p. (andrea.tryphonides@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0652 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank of America-Merrill Lynch cuts \uc2 Hochschild Mining\uc2  (HOC.LN) to neutral from buy. Says its buy rating was based on an expected re-rating of the shares, which broker says has now mostly occurred. "We do remain positive on management's focus on exploration, and the advancement of growth options should provide further upside in the longer term," it adds. Says near-term challenges remain as costs rise and production declines at the group's existing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "We lower our net-present-vaue estimate today by 9% on lower production assumptions. We set a new price objective of 425p from 435p," adds BofA-\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2 . Shares closed at 415p Tuesday. (ishaq.siddiqi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0649 GMT [Dow Jones] Chemring's (CHG.LN) trading update for the first four months of the 2H is reassuring, says Investec, with management maintaining its outlook for the full year. Says any concerns raised at the interims should be dispelled by this statement which indicates improving trading across a number areas. Says Chemring is the highest growth stock in the sector, with potential for further upgrades. Has a buy rating, with a target of 4000p. Shares closed Wednesday at 2950p. (peter.nurse@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0642 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  raises Invensys (ISYS.LN) to buy from neutral, and lifts its target price to 424p from 351p. Says the company provides attractive exposure to structural growth opportunities in the automation and power end markets through its Invensys \b \highlight22\uc2 Operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  Management business. "Free from balance sheet concerns, we believe the company should be able to deliver better growth relative to the market in this cycle versus the last one," it says. Adds the rail signaling business should continue to benefit from structural growth in the rail end market, and expects a lower impact from fiscal austerity measures in this part of the rail market compared with rolling stock. Shares closed at 277p. (ishaq.siddiqi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0638 GMT [Dow Jones] Kingfisher's (KGF.LN) 1H results show adjusted pretax profit up by 23%, largely due to self-help initiatives says an analyst. This is decent, but the analyst thinks the market will focus on management's comments regarding the consumer environment. There are concerns about the fragility of the consumer market and it looks like bottom- line growth will be driven by margin improvements. Shares closed Wednesday at 218.9p. (andrea.tryphonides@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0636 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds open lower, catching up with post-settlement declines of Wednesday ahead of heavy supply later this session. On offer: EUR4B worth of Spanish paper and French nominal bonds and linkers worth up to EUR10.3B. The former auction seen as a key test of appetite for peripherals. No major euro zone data due Thursday, although US jobless claims come at 1230 GMT and Philadelphia Fed survey at 1400 GMT. Trading's likely to remain volatile. "The market appears to be having difficulties reconciling prevailing levels against evolving fundamentals and this is not something we see changing in the near future," says Nomura. December bunds down 0.22 at 129.89 10-year bund down at 98.48 yielding 2.424%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0636 GMT [Dow Jones] RBI surprises analysts by raising key borrowing rate 50 bps; as expected, lifts key lending rate 25 bps as it seeks to temper price pressures in rapidly growing economy. Ups repurchase rate to 6.00%, reverse repo to 5.00%. Move has backing of government as finance minister said Thursday he wasn't satisfied with current level of inflation. But rate hike cycle may be close to over; "the RBI believes the tightening that has been carried out over this period has taken the monetary situation close to normal," bank says in statement. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0632 GMT [Dow Jones] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stance to curb the franc's advance cannot be compared with the Bank of Japan's action Wednesday to check the yen's gains, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "The market took on the SNB with good reason as its economy was stronger than those in the euro region and it has a much stronger fiscal position," says strategist Greg Gibbs. By contrast "Japan's economy is suffering, its stock market is underperforming and its mired in deflation," he says. EUR/CHF at 1.3013 from a low of 1.2987. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0629 GMT [Dow Jones] While BOJ seeming to leave JPY-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unsterilized could help weaken JPY, what Japanese authorities really need is stronger recovery in U.S. economy, says Yuji Kameoka, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daiwa Institute of Research\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "There could be some yen weakening effect given that the Bank of Japan is increasing yen liquidity supply by (so far) not sterilizing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  funds," Kameoka says. But intermittent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could take time to lead to more JPY weakness; better fix would be stronger U.S. economy. "If the U.S. economic outlook improves, pushing up U.S. interest rates, a fundamental shift toward yen weakness and dollar strength would not be far off."(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0626 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly lower; Nikkei flat, S&P/ASX 200 off 1.2%, Kospi down 0.7%, Shanghai Composite 2.3% lower, HSI fell 0.5%, Taiex off 0.8%, STI down 0.3%, SET +0.4%, NZX-50 flat. Malaysia markets shut for holiday. In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, JPY gained tad vs EUR, USD as Japanese authorities appeared to stay out of the markets today after they stepped in to weaken JPY sharply Wednesday. USD/JPY at 85.31 vs 85.63 late NY trade Wednesday; EUR/JPY at 110.80 vs 111.36. EUR/USD at 1.2988 vs 1.3012. BOJ Governor Shirakawa says unconventional monetary easing measures were "highly effective" against "acute pains" in recent financial crisis. BOJ pumped liquidity into banking system, in sign it is trying to help government weaken JPY by leaving cash from Wednesday's massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in money market; \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  injected Y1 trillion into system vs Y700 billion infusion market expected, after skipping its morning \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Japan July Tertiary Industry Activity Index +1.6% on-month seasonally-adjusted to 98.9 vs +0.2% in June. Bloomberg, citing person familiar with matter, reported that China Banking Regulatory Commission may require country's biggest lenders to boost their capital adequacy ratio to as high as 15% by end-2012 vs 11.5% currently. Australian August merchandise imports fell to A$17.58 billion seasonally-adjusted vs A$18.80 billion in July. Thai Industries Sentiment Index fell to 102.4 points in August vs 108.6 points in July. RBNZ keeps official cash rate steady at 3.0%, highlighting ongoing economic uncertainty in light of recent 7.0 magnitude earthquake in Christchurch. Spot gold at $1,265.50 per troy ounce, down $2.70 from NY Wednesday. October crude oil futures down $1.00 at $75.02/bbl. (wei-zhe.tan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0618 GMT [Dow Jones] December bunds are down 0.25 at 129.86, and WestLB says the technical picture remains fragile. The bank sees crucial support at 129.53, the low from Sept. 13, which shields the Aug. 11 low at 129.01. Resistance is at 130.27, 130.41, and 130.56. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0616 GMT [Dow Jones] India interbank call rate, collateralized lending and borrowing obligation rate rises above RBI's key lending rate of 5.75% on expectations \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may lift key rates at policy meeting (due 0630GMT), says state-run bank trader. Overnight call rate at 6.00% vs 5.75% last close, CBLO rate at 5.78% vs 5.75% at open. Trader says, banks also borrowing heavily as corporates withdraw funds on last day of advance tax payment Thursday. "The market is pricing in at least a 25 basis points rate hike and lenders have positioned themselves accordingly. If RBI does lift rates, we can see the call rate touching 6.25% in session," trader says. Notes, banks also borrowing heavily from RBI's daily discount lending window. Tips call rate in 5.75%-6.25% band in session. Indian banks borrowed INR518.50 billion from RBI Thursday. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com) [ 16-09-10 0705GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0614 GMT [Dow Jones] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  is widely expected to keep its key interest rate on hold Thursday, with market players focusing instead on its outlooks for growth and inflation, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "The recent franc gains may provoke renewed SNB concerns on deflation," says Lutz Karpowitz. The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Wednesday to check yen gains has stirred speculation of similar SNB actions, but \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  notes the two scenarios cannot be compared as the Swiss economy is booming and the risks of deflation have receded. "Should the SNB raise the issue of deflation however, this could weigh on the Swiss franc," he notes. EUR/CHF at 1.3008 from a previous close of 1.3042.(neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0612 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's economy is more dependent on China than ever, and little can be done to guard against uncertainties of that connection, says Philip Lowe, Assistant Governor of RBA in afternoon speech. "Clearly what happens in the Australian economy is now more dependent upon what happens in China than has been the case at any time in our past," he says. Still, assistant governor says improving China's data collection and dissemination process was key, signalling Australia's statistics bureau and central were looking to help in that process. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0610 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia government bonds extend winning streak to 2 sessions as foreign investors continue to buy after week-long holiday, traders say. "Trade volume is lighter compared with yesterday but foreign funds are still driving prices higher," trader says. Still, "yields, especially for the 10- and 15-year bonds, may rise tomorrow ahead of the finance ministry's bond auction (next) Tuesday." 5-year yield steady at 7.38%, 10-year yield at 7.91% vs 7.93% yesterday; 15-year at 8.45% vs 8.51%; 20-year at 8.88% vs 8.98%; 28-year at 9.18% vs 9.26%. Finance ministry plans to sell IDR3 trillion worth of papers maturing in 1-, 11-, 16-, and 21-year on Sept. 21.(andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0604 GMT [Dow Jones] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  is likely to "play it safe" Thursday, and continue with its low interest rate policy due to the strong franc and the danger of a slowdown in global economic growth, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Sarasin\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "Swiss exports have weathered the franc's appreciation surprising well, the risk of a second global recession entails the danger of a further contraction in the Swiss economy, and with very low price pressure it can err on the side of expansive rather than tight policy," economist Alessandro Bee says. The SNB decision on rates is due at 1200 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0638 GMT [Dow Jones] Kingfisher's (KGF.LN) 1H results show adjusted pretax profit up by 23%, largely due to self-help initiatives says an analyst. This is decent, but the analyst thinks the market will focus on management's comments regarding the consumer environment. There are concerns about the fragility of the consumer market and it looks like bottom- line growth will be driven by margin improvements. Shares closed Wednesday at 218.9p. (andrea.tryphonides@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0638 GMT [Dow Jones] Kingfisher (KGF.LN) 1H results are better than expected, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Arden Partners\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst Nick Bubb. Adjusted pretax profit is up 23% to GBP354M, beating Bubb's forecast of GBP340M. "The statement reads well...and [the results] look strong across the board." Has an 'add' rating. Shares closed 3.3% higher Tuesday at 219p. (vladimir.guevarra@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0636 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  upgrades \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cable & Wireless Worldwide\f28 \uc2 \f2  (CW.LN) to buy from neutral, and lifts target to 100p from 96p. Says CWW is a mean reversion, earnings momentum story with M&A potential. Says "investor sentiment is low, fears on UK Enterprise high, and that austerity pressures are priced in, which provides an exciting entry point." Also initiates Talk Talk (TALK.LN) at neutral with a 155p target. Says overall trends are positive, but it does not deliver enough upside to be more positive on the shares. CWW shares closed at 74p, and Talk Talk at 138p. (peter.nurse@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0629 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  downgrades \f28 \uc2 \f2 BT Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BT) to equalweight from overweight and target to 155p from 190p. Thinks it possible there could be a further statement from the Pensions Regulator this autumn. Warns if the regulator proposes a quicker deficit reduction schedule of 10 years rather than 17, this would imply no spare free cash flow beyond pension top-ups and dividend. Also has concerns with regard to BT's exposure to UK government contracts. Estimates a drag on FY '12 Ebita of around GBP45M and GBP60M in FY '13. Says this is manageable but challenges the recent upward trend in estimates and BT's top line growth strategy for FY '13. Shares closed 144p.(andrea.tryphonides@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0625 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  initiates \f28 \uc2 \f2 Smiths Group (SMIN.LN) \f28 \uc2 \f2 at neutral with a 1350p target. Says the company has long been seen as a potential candidate for being broken up, but \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  sees no immediate catalyst for this and consequently no reason for an imminent return of cash to shareholders. "A product focus on operating expenditure has strong defensive traits but add this to relatively small emerging market exposure, and growth prospects seem limited versus the broader sector," added \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Shares closed at 1223p. (ishaq.siddiqi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9288 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 16-09-10 0705GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1113   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100916e69g000hh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
