\par\par \b \uc2 BASE METALS: LME Metals Drift Lower In Listless Asian Trade\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Fickling   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 653 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 08:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY (Dow Jones)--Base metals drifted lower in Asian trade on the London Metal Exchange Thursday, but many traders were barely active ahead of holidays in Asia and with regional investors digesting the implications of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It's 1.20 p.m. and we've only just clicked over 600 lots of copper"--equivalent to 15,000 metric tons of the most-traded LME metal in more than four hours of trading, said a trader for a European trading house in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 "Copper's just dead today," said another trader in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 As many as 2,815 lots of copper had traded in a single hour last Thursday, when reports--later denied--of Chinese authorities investigating a rubber futures broker prompted a rush of liquidation.\par \par \uc2 Lead, nickel and tin were the biggest fallers today, all losing 1.2%-1.3% against Wednesday's PM kerb by 0600 GMT.\par \par \uc2 Tin had hit a fresh 24-month record of $23,200 during European trading hours Thursday, following a steady climb in recent months.\par \par \uc2 In line with the direction of Asian trade in recent months, zinc was again the most-traded metal thanks to high levels of activity on the Shanghai Futures Exchange.\par \par \uc2 At 0530 GMT, the most-traded January zinc on the SHFE was down CNY55 at CNY17,685/ton, a 2.3% premium to LME 3-month futures, which stood at $2,135/ton at 0600 GMT.\par \par \uc2 December SHFE copper fell CNY200 to CNY58,890, a 4.5% discount to the LME 3-month's $7,580/ton.\par \par \uc2 Investors speculated that Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be contributing to the sluggish activity in the region.\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent around 1 trillion yen to buy U.S. dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in an effort to depress the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and make the country's exports cheaper for foreign buyers.\par \par \uc2 The move also sparked a rise in the trade-weighted U.S. dollar index. Upward moves in the dollar tend to depress the prices of commodities priced in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But such \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are notoriously expensive to sustain, and many investors question whether Bank of Japan would be able to hold back appreciation of the yen.\par \par \uc2 "The talk has been that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't actually work anyway so the metals have all remained stuck in the higher end of their ranges," said the Hong Kong-based trader. "It needs to be a bit clearer on the macro side before the base metals push out of these ranges."\par \par \uc2 Another trader from a major regional trading house said his company's Tokyo office had hardly been active in the market all day.\par \par \uc2 Data due out later in the day include U.K. retail sales for August and an industrial trends survey for September, the E.U.'s July trade balance, and U.S. producer prices for August.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary Tim Geithner is due to speak before a Congressional hearing focusing on Chinese exchange rate policy during U.S. trading hours.\par \par \uc2 The following are LME three-month base metals' prices at 0600 GMT and changes versus Wednesday's afternoon kerb:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par LME 3-Mo  Last        Change   % Change  Prev PM Kerb \par Copper    $7,580.00  -$35.00  -0.5%      $7,615.00 \par Lead      $2,207.75  -$27.25  -1.2%      $2,235.00 \par Zinc      $2,135.00  -$10.00  -0.5%      $2,145.00 \par Aluminum  $2,138.00  -$15.00  -0.7%      $2,153.00 \par Nickel   $22,900.00  -$300.00 -1.3%     $23,200.00 \par Tin      $22,875.00  -$275.00 -1.2%     $23,150.00 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By David Fickling, Dow Jones Newswires; +61 2 8272 4689; david.fickling@dowjones.com [ 16-09-10 0636GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 61041 82008   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100916e69g000e9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
