\par\par \b \uc2 COLUMN-Speculators, China win big on yen move: James Saft\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jim Saft    \par \uc2 878 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 21:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 (James Saft is a Reuters columnist. The opinions expressed are his own)\par \par \uc2 HUNTSVILLE, Ala. (Reuters) - What does $4 trillion a day in business, never sleeps and sees Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  as just one more patsy?\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, of course, which is licking its collective lips as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  embarks on another round of unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen in an effort to drive down its value and protect its export-oriented economy.\par \par \uc2 There are going to be two big winners in this, and neither begins with a "J."\par \par \uc2 Speculators will, as ever, benefit from having a deep-pocketed trading partner who has been so kind as to draw a bull's-eye on his own forehead and tell everyone at what level he will act. For Japan, that level appears to be just below 83 yen to the dollar and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already spent $20 billion moving it back to above 85 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Not a bad first day's effort but let us recall that the last time Japan decided to mess around in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets without international support it ended up spending well over $300 billion between 2003-2004, a period when the yen actually appreciated by more than 13 percent.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland engaged in a similarly painful exercise in driving down the value of its franc earlier this year, shelling out something on the order of $210 billion but seeing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  actually increase in value against the euro, its main trading partner, by about 14 percent.\par \par \uc2 About the only thing that can be said in favor of Japan's action is that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not act to neutralize the effect of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the money supply, unlike in previous \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This will increase liquidity and should be inflationary, a welcome effect in the land of falling prices.\par \par \uc2 As for international success, so far the signs are not promising:\par \par \uc2 "Unilateral actions are not the appropriate way to deal with global imbalances," Jean-Claude Juncker, chairman of the Eurogroup of euro zone finance ministers, said.\par \par \uc2 Even more astounding, a European Union source told Reuters that Japan had not even told the Europeans, much less the United States, about its plans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There is an excellent reason for that: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had no hope in heaven that calls for a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would meet with anything but refusal. Quite the opposite, both the United States and Europe would doubtless like nothing better than for their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall in value, thus supporting their own struggling economies. Do you think Greece wants a weaker yen? What about Michigan?\par \par \uc2 CHINA PLAYS A BEAUTY\par \par \uc2 The fact is that unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the current circumstances is nothing less than a tactic of a trade war, in deed if not in name.\par \par \uc2 So who is the other big winner in Japan's latest escapade? None other than China, which by buying Japanese debt has helped to precipitate the rise in the yen and now sees Japan do its dollar buying for it. Brilliant.\par \par \uc2 University of Oregon economist and noted Federal Reserve watcher Tim Duy presciently wrote about this possibility in August:\par \par \uc2 "Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if it occurs, means that Chinese authorities managed to get Japan to acquire their dollar reserves for them. Instead of buying dollars, China buys yen, which in turn induces Japan to buy dollars. This maintains the artificial capital flows to the U.S. while allowing China to escape accusations of being a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator" <{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://economistsview.typepad.com/timduy/2010/09/yen-intervention.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://economistsview.typepad.com/timduy/2010/09/yen-\b  \highlight22intervention\b0 \highlight22 .html}}}\uc2 ?>;\par \par \uc2 You do have to have sympathy for the Japanese authorities: they face deflation, a weak economy and political ructions at home. What's more, they must see the possibility for further quantitative easing in the United States and realize that this will only strengthen the yen further.\par \par \uc2 Being expected to pay the price for China's reserve diversification must have been the last straw.\par \par \uc2 While China has done well on its own account, its actions have greatly increased the chances of protectionism and trade wars, or rather the chances that its own unilateral trade war will become generalized. The yuan has barely strengthened against the dollar since the much vaunted liberalization in June.\par \par \uc2 Sooner or later this must become intolerable to the U.S. administration, which is sailing into an election with high unemployment and a low approval rating. It should come as little surprise that the United States moved on Wednesday to bring two new cases against China before the World Trade Organization, one on steel and the other in banking.\par \par \uc2 As the year winds down, expect more of the same, and for it to be bad for growth and for stocks.\par \par \uc2 Until then, drinks are on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  desks and the speculators. (Editing by James Dalgleish) (At the time of publication James Saft did not own any direct investments in securities mentioned in this article. He may be an owner indirectly as an investor in a fund. You can email him at jamessaft@jamessaft.com and find more columns at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.reuters.com/news/globalcoverage/columns"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.reuters.com/news/globalcoverage/columns}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 COLUMN-MARKETS/SAFT|LANGEN|FUN    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100916e69g001g1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
