\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Blue Chips Edge Higher, Boosted By Tech Stocks\b0\par\par\uc2 2,497 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 00:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10594.83  +22.10  +0.21% \par Nasdaq     2303.25   +1.93  +0.08% \par S&P 500    1124.66   -0.41  -0.04% \par FTSE 100   5540.14  -15.42  -0.28% \par Xetra DAX  6249.65  -12.22  -0.20% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  99 25/32  flat  0.480% flat \par 5Y Tsy  98 31/32  -3/32 1.467% +2.2BP \par 10Y Tsy 98 25/32 -12/32 2.766% +4.3BP \par  \par USD/JPY       85.90-91     +0.23% \par Range         85.93-85.23 \par EUR/USD       1.3077-79    +0.53% \par Range         1.3117-1.2977 \par AUD/USD       0.9363-69    -0.14% \par Range         0.9395-0.9332 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    11.01-11.39% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    10.55-10.85% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    12.00-12.30% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,273.80 +$5.10 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,266.10-$1,279.50 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $74.57   -$1.45 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Technology companies boosted U.S. blue-chip stocks to a narrow gain Thursday, as confidence mounted that the economy would avoid a double-dip recession.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average closed up 22.10 points, or 0.21%, at 10594.83, its highest close since Aug. 10. Technology stocks led the climb, as investors gained faith that the economic recovery is slowing but not headed toward a second slump. Leading the Dow Industrials' climb, \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  rose 73 cents, or 1.8%, to 40.35, while Cisco Systems gained 34 cents, or 1.6%, to 21.93 and Intel added 25 cents, or 1.3%, to 18.97.\par \par \uc2 "It seems like we priced in too much of a double dip, and it's not coming to pass as bad as people anticipated," said Patrick Becker, Jr., principal at Becker Capital Management.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index slipped 0.41, or 0.04%, to 1124.66. The Nasdaq Composite edged up 1.93, or 0.08%, to 2303.25.\par \par \uc2 While all the benchmark indexes have surged this month, the Nasdaq has outpaced its peers. The measure has climbed 8.95% since the start of September as fears of a double-dip recession have relaxed. The Nasdaq has climbed for seven straight sessions, its longest streak since the seven-day run that ended July 14.\par \par \uc2 "Technology just got walloped, and many of the value-oriented tech names really underperformed in the first half of the year," Becker said. "You do have a valuation bounce occurring in those names."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the DJIA has gained 5.79% this month, while the S&P 500 has added 7.18%.\par \par \uc2 Still, recent muddy economic data have left investors hesitant about the rally's sustainability and kept trading confined to a narrow range. As concerns over the economy have loosened their grip, anxiety rose Thursday over U.S. lawmakers' aggressive stance on China's exchange-rate policy and the potential to strain relations with China.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended at their lowest level in more than three weeks Thursday, led by banks, following a report that Beijing might impose stricter capital requirements on lenders.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index will likely trade between 2550 and 2650 Friday as the market remains concerned about the possibility the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise interest rates as part of its efforts to rein in inflation.\par \par \uc2 The banking regulator later responded to the report on lender requirements, saying it has no new capital adequacy requirement for major banks.\par \par \uc2 "Investors were worried that if Beijing were to introduce a higher CAR, banks may need fresh rounds of financing to meet those requirements," Li Xin, an analyst at China Development Bank Securities, said.\par \par \uc2 "If Beijing doesn't announce changes to interest rates by this weekend, the market may rally sharply next week," said Simon Wang, an analyst at \uc2 Guoyuan Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks ended marginally lower Thursday as exporters surrendered much of their early gains as the yen rose slightly, while commodity-related stocks lost ground on brokerage rating cuts.\par \par \uc2 Market observers say that investors are reluctant to actively trade as they wait to see if Japanese monetary authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets again.\par \par \uc2 "Investors have learned what the downside threshold for dollar/yen will be, but they are trying to ascertain the upside limit," says Shinichiro Matsushita, market analyst at Daiwa Securities. He said that investors foresee \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  taking place when the dollar falls under Y83, but they are still testing how high the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest goes.\par \par \uc2 "I would expect a stock market rebound to be met by selling from next week from foreigners as the outlook for earnings has not changed," said Mattia Ciancaleoni, director of equity sales at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures prices dropped for the third straight day in U.S. trading Thursday as a scheduled restart of a major pipeline rekindled oversupply concerns in the world's biggest oil consumer.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures posted a new record settlement Thursday as the U.S. dollar weakened and investors looked for more of the precious metal as a haven while equities eased.\par \par \uc2 Base metals on the London Metal Exchange mostly closed marginally higher Thursday after drifting in recent ranges for much of the session, with mixed U.S. data failing to provide clear direction.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar held onto its sharp gains against the yen a day after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to strangle the recent strength in its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's estimated Y2 trillion \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has left \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  investors cautious, with Japanese officials saying more action could be on the table to stem the yen's strength, a stance that might have also given a boost to the euro, said Steven Englander, head of G10 strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If the market becomes convinced that [Japan] will be resisting U.S. dollar weakness against the yen, other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will become more attractive as U.S. dollar alternatives," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After Wednesday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , one of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's biggest daily yen-selling, dollar-buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Prime Minister Naoto Kan reiterated Thursday that the country will continue to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market when necessary.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Thursday in New York, the euro was at $1.3085, up from $1.3012 late Wednesday, according to EBS via CQG, off its intraday high of $1.3117 but still up more than 0.5% on the day. The dollar was at Y85.81, up from Y85.63, while the euro was at Y112.29, up from Y111.36. The U.K. pound was at $1.5635, up from $1.5623. The dollar was at CHF1.0146, up from CHF1.0035.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 81.224, down from 81.453.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro Thursday climbed to its strongest level against the dollar in more than a month on the heels of a well-received auction of Spanish government debt that helped the region's debt crisis fade further into the background. The debt sale followed successful auctions earlier this week in Italy and Greece.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors may also see the euro as a sensible temporary safety play, said Alan Ruskin, global head of G-10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York, as concerns mount that the Federal Reserve will implement a fresh round of asset purchases, known as quantitative easing, to jolt a slowing economy. Further loosened monetary policy would weigh on the greenback, with investors using cheap dollars to fund bets on higher-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With investors also cautious about any further moves by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to temper yen strength after Wednesday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the euro might offer the best opportunity to express antidollar sentiment, Ruskin said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But "long-term sentiment toward the euro is still very negative," Ruskin said, with the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unlikely to muster a gain beyond $1.35.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro, along with the dollar, also shot higher against the Swiss franc, each gaining more than 1%, after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  Thursday left key interest rates unchanged, noting weakening inflation and a slowdown in growth for the rest of 2010 and in 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner said in Congressional testimony Thursday that China's move toward a more flexible exchange rate has been "too slow," though Geithner signalled the Obama administration was reluctant to formally label China a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulator under U.S. law. China's yuan rose overnight to its strongest level since it began trading in 1994, the fourth consecutive session in which the yuan hit a record intraday high.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys maturing in five or more years fell Thursday, hurt by selling related to new corporate and government agency debt supply.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also, bonds weakened as the latest round of U.S. data Thursday eased speculation the Federal Reserve may conduct larger-scale government bond purchases.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10- and 30-year Treasurys bore the brunt of the selling. The 30-year bond yield touched 3.959%, the highest level since Aug. 13.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These long-dated maturities extended losses made Wednesday, as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's first \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in six years spurred many investors to shift money into short-dated notes that are believed to be Japan's favored places to park its dollar proceeds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There are so many cross currents," and Treasurys are "the whipping post," said Thomas Roth, a senior Treasury trader at Mitsubishi UFJ Securities (USA) in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 1955 GMT, the benchmark 10-year Treasury note was down 12/32 in price and its yield rose to 2.766%. The 30-year bond was down 30/32 to yield 3.931%. Bond yields move inversely to prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Short-dated Treasurys outperformed, with the two-year note trading flat to yield 0.480%. The yield premium of the 10-year note over the two-year note rose to 229 basis points, the highest level since Aug. 10, from 223 basis points a day earlier.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Supply of new corporate and agency debt has been brisk this month as sellers take advantage of low interest rates to refinance debt. Among the most notable sources of new debt supply is the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tennessee Valley Authority\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a federally chartered company. The TVA sold a $1 billion 50-year bond Thursday. [ 16-09-10 2204GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trading in the bond market has been choppy in recent sessions as a rally has faded. Many data released this month have been less dire than forecast, easing worries about the U.S. economy headed into a double-dip recession in the second half of the year. That cast doubt on whether the Fed plans to carry out larger-scale purchases in Treasurys in coming months to support the economy, and Thursday's data helped ease such speculation further.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The latest U.S. weekly jobless claims posted an unexpected drop, showing some hope for the labor market, while the business outlook index from the Federal Reserve Bank of Philadelphia was below zero for a second month and worse than forecast. Meanwhile, wholesale prices rose last month but remained tame.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended lower Thursday amid increasing liquidity pressure ahead of two long public holidays, when the public's demand for cash typically rises--the Mid-Autumn Festival runs Sept. 22-24 and the National Day holiday Oct. 1-7.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Investors that have funded their bond purchases via repo transaction are cutting debt holdings as banks have been reluctant to lend them money before the holidays," a Shanghai-based trader with a local insurer said, tipping the Shanghai exchange government bond index in a 126.6-126.8 range in coming sessions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds rose Thursday as investors interpreted the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's decision to inject funds into the money market as a sign that it's loosening monetary conditions to help the government fight the strong yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The result of the Bank of Japan's money market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Thursday means that the BOJ has so far avoided draining funds from the government's yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Wednesday, estimated by some market participants at a record Y2 trillion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has shifted the BOJ's policy direction to further policy easing," said Tetsuya Miura, the chief market analyst at Mizuho Securities. "The bond market is probably the one that benefits the most from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition to possible unsterilized \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the BOJ may ease its monetary policy further next month, said RuiXue Xu, rates strategist at RBS Securities Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Now it's more likely that the government will put more pressure on the BOJ to take more easing steps" at the next scheduled policy meeting between Oct. 4 and Oct. 5, she said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Xu said the benchmark 10-year yield may fall to 1.000% down the road, helped by buy-on-dip demand from banks to short- to medium-term sovereign bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among the data due Friday, exports from Singapore likely continued to grow at a double-digit rate in August, though at a slower pace than in previous months as the base effect diminishes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Non-oil domestic exports are expected to have risen 14.9% on the year compared with a 18.2% rise in July, a Dow Jones Newswires poll of 6 economists shows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Compared with the previous month, August exports are expected to have declined 1.5% in seasonally adjusted terms.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US Jobless Claims Drop Spurs Recovery Hopes \par Geithner Critical Of Pace Of Yuan Hike;US Lawmakers Seek Action \par WSJ: China Yuan Rise Fuels Thoughts Of Heading Off Backlash In US \par RIM Profit, Shipment Top Forecasts, Shares Surge \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2  Profit Up 20% On Hardware Revenues \par US Senate Passes Small Business Bill \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fedex\f28 \uc2 \f2  Gives Cautious Near-term Outlook \par Total CEO Sees Delays For Gulf Project Approvals \par ACS Plans Bid For Majority Of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hochtief\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par Carrefour To Spend EUR1.5B On European Store \par UK Proposes Insolvency Scheme For Investment Banks \par =China MediaExpress Plans $30M Share Buyback >CCME \par S Korea Department Store Sales Up 8.5% On Year In August; +10.1% July \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2200  New Zealand  3Q  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  McDermott Miller Consumer Confidence \par                    Index \par 0300  S Korea      Aug Corporate Bankruptcies \par 0500  Japan        Aug Steel Production \par 0500  Singapore    Aug Merchandise Trade \par 0530  Japan        Aug Tokyo area department store sales \par 0530  Japan        Aug Nationwide department store sales \par 0600  Germany      Aug PPI \par 0730  Thailand     Weekly International Reserves \par 0800  EU           Jul Euro area balance of payments \par 0830  UK           2Q  BoE on UK banks external claims \par 0900  EU           Jul Construction output \par 1130  India        Weekly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves \par 1230  US           Aug Real Earnings \par 1230  US           Aug CPI \par 1355  US           Sep Thomson Reuters / University of Michigan Survey of \pa