\par\par \b \uc2 HEARD ON THE STREET: Daily Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 1,765 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 17:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =HEARD ON THE STREET: Tokyo's Yen Shift Poses Trader Puzzle \par  \par    By Richard Barley and Simon Nixon \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 How should investors respond to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's surprise \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb the strength of the yen? That depends largely on whether the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  turns out to be a political gesture, limited to the roughly Y2 trillion so far spent; or something more like the 2003-2004 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the tune of Y35 trillion. Western policymakers, believing the exercise to be misguided and potentially damaging, will be hoping for the former; but Tokyo certain has room in its budget plans to fund a bigger program.\f28 \par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the initial impact on exchange rates other than dollar-yen has been limited. While the yen has fallen 3.3% against the dollar, the dollar hasn't gained against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But if the yen is no longer a one-way trade, investors could switch to commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Australian dollar, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . If dollar weakness causes emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gain, the risk is that central banks elsewhere in Asia and Latin America also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo's move is also likely to affect fixed-income markets, particularly as the Bank of Japan seems intent on leaving the funds in the market, making this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  a form of quantitative easing as well. Japanese government bond yields fell Wednesday and could fall further if more liquidity floods into the market. But it will be crucial to watch Japanese fiscal policy, since further quantitative easing could increase sovereign credit risk. Japanese sovereign credit-default swaps have so far remained flat at 67 basis points.\par \par \uc2 Of course, it's possible a lower yen will succeed in boosting Japanese exporters, which over time will be reflected in investors switching out of bonds into equities. But exports have never been Japan's problem: the real issue is the lack of domestic demand. And on that score, Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , by reducing the value of domestic earnings, may be self-defeating. That would point to Japan's actions being a political gesture, albeit a futile one. Tokyo's rhetoric suggests otherwise.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =HEARD ON THE STREET: China's Unruly Auto Sector \par  \par    By Andrew Peaple \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Beijing is playing a now-familiar record: China's auto industry needs to get leaner. The rest of the country isn't listening.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 According to J.D. Power, nearly 80 different manufacturers compete in the light vehicle market alone. The central government has long desired some rationalization of this sprawl, and this week state media reported that China's State Council has placed the auto sector atop a list of industries needing consolidation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It isn't the first time the authorities have made such a call. Last year, Beijing unveiled a grand plan to develop a core of auto groups to lead the domestic industry. Since then, there's hardly been an avalanche of activity. The last big deal, Changan Auto's purchase of Hafei Auto and Changhe Auto, was announced in late in 2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Beijing's logic is clear. A more streamlined sector will enable the larger companies to benefit from economies of scale and increased pricing power, in turn raising their profits available for re-investment. Ultimately, that could help China develop a clutch of world-class car makers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But it seems this isn't swaying local governments which house the auto makers. In a growing market where even small car makers are doing well, they provide employment, a measure of economic growth, and a source of tax revenue to local authorities. The type of consolidation Beijing wants could see some plants slimmed down or closed, with provinces in turn losing those benefits.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The resistance is justified, for now, by China's fast-rising auto sales. Year-to-year growth may be slowing, but auto sales were still up 48% in the first half from a year earlier. Few analysts see anything other than continued expansion of car ownership in China in the coming years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The central planners, then, are in need of a good crisis. A drop in auto sales, which would reveal high levels of industry overcapacity and lead to losses among those too small to operate profitably in a slower growth environment, would bolster its scheme. At the moment that seems far off.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Beijing may hope its car industry develops like the U.S.'s in the 20th century, with a core of leading firms emerging from an initial plethora.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But then, that process did take about a century.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =HEARD ON THE STREET: Starz Could Become A Black Hole For Netflix \par  \par    By Martin Peers \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the battle for digital control of Hollywood, Netflix (NFLX) has plenty of Epix. But can it keep Starz?\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That question looms for anyone pondering whether to buy Netflix shares, trading near $145 a share or roughly 39 times expected 2011 earnings. Last month's Epix licensing agreement gives Netflix new titles and lots of older movies from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Paramount Pictures\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Lions Gate (LGF) and MGM. It also suggests Netflix will have to pay much more to renew its existing deal with Starz.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That's an issue. Liberty Media (LMCB)-owned Starz licenses movies from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sony\f28 \uc2 \f2  (6758.TO, SNE) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Walt Disney Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (DIS) for its cable channels. By giving Netflix access to that content in 2008, Starz put Netflix's streaming service on the map. Wall Street firm Morgan Keegan estimated in a report Monday that Starz accounted for half of the 30 most popular streaming titles on Netflix, based on data from a website that tracks the streaming service.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And it comes cheap. While the precise price hasn't been disclosed, Wall Street analysts estimate Netflix pays $20 million to $30 million annually for Starz. Given Netflix is paying Epix roughly $200 million a year--for more titles but fewer new ones--the Starz price is probably the bargain of the decade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Last decade, that is. The Starz deal is expected to come up for renewal in the next year and it's reasonable to assume Starz will ask for a massive increase. Already, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Disney\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Starz renegotiated their agreement to ensure \f28 \uc2 \f2 Disney\f28 \uc2 \f2  has the chance to earn more from the Netflix streaming service.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If Netflix loses Starz, it has nowhere else to go for streaming content from major Hollywood studios. Aside from Epix and Starz-licensed studios, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Time Warner\f28 \uc2 \f2 's (TWX) HBO has locked up other Hollywood majors under long-term deals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Admittedly, with Epix and some smaller deals, Netflix isn't as dependent on Starz as it once was. It says its streaming service now offers 46% of 2010 box office releases, which it estimates is more than \f28 \uc2 \f2 Time Warner\f28 \uc2 \f2 's HBO. But that percentage includes Starz.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Netflix bulls play all this down, arguing the content helps drive continued rapid subscriber growth. Indeed Netflix expects to finish this year with around 18 million subscribers, up from 12.2 million at the end of 2009. \f28 \uc2 \f2 JP Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  estimates Netflix could reach 29.5 million subscribers by the end of 2012. That implies revenue will double from last year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But maintaining past growth rates on a bigger subscriber base won't be easy. And even if Netflix can pull that off, the bull argument assumes Netflix's DVD and mailing costs for its core DVD mail-order service increase more slowly, as subscribers shift away from DVDs to streaming. Yet with Netflix's streaming service still missing much of Hollywood's best output, the DVDs will likely remain crucial for subscribers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Should Netflix's viewers continue seeing Starz, investors might unhappily do the same.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =HEARD ON THE STREET: Coal Isn't Burned Out Just Yet \par  \par    By Liam Denning \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The future is...coal? Energy expert Daniel Yergin reckons coal will remain the top fuel for power plants globally for the next two decades.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Output from sources like solar power is growing faster in percentage terms, but remains tiny in absolute terms. Coal is the second largest source of energy overall and average demand growth over the past five years was 3.5%, much faster than for oil or natural gas. The extra coal the world burned in 2009 relative to 2004 was roughly equivalent to the entire energy consumption of Germany and France combined last year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Coal benefits from three things. Unlike oil, coal reserves are widely distributed. Coal is also relatively cheap, calorie for calorie, averaging about one-third the price of oil and less than half the price of natural gas over the past 15 years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Coal's third advantage isn't inherent to it. Rather, it benefits from faltering momentum towards putting a price on carbon emissions in the U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. carbon-pricing remains likely at some point, so investment in new coal-fired power plants should stay on hold. Investment bank Tudor, Pickering, Holt & Co. expects coal-fired generation capacity to peak in 2011. Even if that limits growth in U.S. coal demand, though, in the absence of a carbon price it shouldn't fall either.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, other markets are growing, most notably China, which accounts for about half of global coal demand already. Besides power generation, China is a big user of higher-priced metallurgical coal, used to make steel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another country to watch is India. It accounted for just 7.5% of global coal consumption last year. But per capita usage is less than a fifth that of China, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2 , suggesting huge potential.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This Asian opportunity, together with regulatory uncertainty in the U.S., should aid two stocks, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Peabody Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BTU) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Consol Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  (CNX).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Peabody is active in Australia, particularly mining metallurgical coal, which accounted for 3% of last year's sales by volume, but 23% of revenue. Peabody tried to buy Australian miner \f28 \uc2 \f2 Macarthur Coal\f28 \uc2 \f2  (MCC.AU) earlier this year, but eventually walked away. Still, the company's effort to diversify away from home is strategically sound. [ 16-09-10 1542GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consol, meanwhile, produces not just coal, but also gas, which accounts for roughly a fifth of earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortization.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consol's mix means that if coal comes under regulatory pressure in the U.S., demand for its other product, gas, should rise. Alternatively, if gas prices rise, demand for coal should increase as some power generators switch to the cheaper fuel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such hedged bets make sense if coal remains king, but one living on borrowed time. [ 16-09-10 1542GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4257   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100916e69g0015x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
