\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,376 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 13:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOF IKEDA: TOKYO WON'T RUN OUT OF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FUNDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--A senior Japanese vice finance minister said Thursday the government won't run of money needed to continue \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, countering speculation that the nation's aggressive efforts to weaken the yen are unsustainable. (published 1039 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ GOVERNOR: READY TO DO MORE IN FACE OF ECONOMIC DOWNSIDE RISK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa reiterated Thursday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is ready to implement further monetary policy steps, as the bank needs to pay more attention to downside risks to the domestic economy. (published 0715 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US EXPORT PLAN HONES IN ON SMALL BUSINESSES, DEVELOPING NATIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. cabinet secretaries laid out a plan Thursday to double exports by focusing largely on small businesses and developing countries, even as the administration steps up efforts to gain access to China and other markets. (published 1000 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EURO ZONE POSTED TRADE SURPLUS IN JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro zone posted a larger-than-expected trade surplus in July as imports fell more rapidly than exports. (published 0900 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ECONOMICS MINISTER BRUEDERLE: GERMAN 2010 GDP CLEARLY ABOVE +2.5% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's economy could grow clearly above 2.5% this year, Germany's Economics Minister Rainer Bruederle said Thursday, adding that the recovery will deliver an upswing in employment. (published 0733 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPANISH AUCTIONS HIGHLIGHT GROWING SPLIT IN EURO-ZONE PERIPHERY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The split between the euro zone's weakest peripheral countries has been highlighted by robust demand at Thursday's Spanish auctions. (published 1044 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN LABOR MINISTER: UNEMPLOYMENT POSSIBLY BELOW 3M BY YEAR-END \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German unemployment levels will probably fall below 3 million by the end of the year, German Labor Minster Ursula von der Leyen said Thursday, highlighting Germany's surprisingly solid labor market. (published 0936 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ: EXPECT CURRENT ACCOUNT BALANCE TO RISE Y2 TLN FRI VS Y15.09 TLN THU \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan forecast its current account balance will increase by Y2 trillion Friday, indicating it plans to help the government weaken the yen by leaving cash from Wednesday's massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market. (published 0758 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VICE FINANCE MINISTER: WON'T RUN OUT OF MONEY FOR \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese senior vice finance minister Motohisa Ikeda said Thursday the government will not run out of funds needed to continue \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. (published 0911 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OBAMA ADMINISTRATION SEES LIMITED JOB LOSS FROM OFFSHORE OIL BAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Obama administration's six-month moratorium on offshore oil drilling in the Gulf of Mexico has had a limited effect on employment despite proclamations the ban would cripple the already fragile coastal economies. (published 1000 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UK YEAR-AHEAD CPI EXPECTATIONS HIGHEST SINCE AUG 2008 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. public inflation expectations for the year ahead rose to their highest level in two years in August, as actual inflation stayed well above target and the impact of hot weather on food prices hit the headlines. (published 0836 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UK AUG RETAIL SALES WEAKEN AHEAD OF GOVERNMENT CUTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. retail sales fell in August to their lowest level since January, confounding expectations for growth, with consumers seemingly wary ahead of the government's planned spending cuts. (published 0927 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: UK SEPT INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT +12 VS +10 IN AUG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. industrial output growth strengthened in September, driven by reasonable export orders, the Confederation of British Industry said Thursday.(published 1000 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA REGULATOR: NO NEW CAPITAL ADEQUACY REQUIREMENT FOR BIG BANKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's banking regulator said Thursday it has no new capital adequacy requirement for major banks following media reports it is considering raising the capital requirement for the country's biggest lenders to as high as 15%. (published 0636 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ LEAVES SOME \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FUNDS IN MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pumped liquidity into the banking system Thursday, in a sign it is trying to help the government weaken the yen by leaving cash from Wednesday's massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the money market. (published 0528 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GEITHNER: 'VERY CONCERNED' ABOUT CHINA'S \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  POLICY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's move toward a more flexible exchange rate has been "too slow," Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner will tell U.S. lawmakers Thursday, calling on a more balanced relationship between the two powers. (published 0022 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA YUAN HITS NEW HIGH AHEAD OF GEITHNER TESTIMONY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan hit another high against the U.S. dollar Thursday, despite the dollar's sharp rebound in international markets overnight, ahead of U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner's testimony at a Congressional hearing on China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy. (published 0507 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA MAY REQUIRE 15% CAPITAL RATIO FOR BIGGEST LENDERS - BLOOMBERG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's banking regulator may require the country's biggest lenders to boost their capital adequacy ratio to as high as 15% by the end of 2012, Bloomberg reported Thursday, citing a person familiar with the matter. (published 0403 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FRENCH BUDGET MIN: MAY OUTDO 2010 DEFICIT AIM; SHOWS CRISIS EXIT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French Budget Minister Francois Baroin Thursday reiterated recent government comments that it could outperform its 2010 deficit target, saying this shows the country's economy to be emerging from the crisis. (published 0702 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EU MINISTERS AGREE ON SOUTH KOREA TRADE DEAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European Union's trade ministers Thursday approved a major agreement with South Korea that will eliminate most trade tariffs and streamline regulations that have hindered trade between the two regions. (published 1005 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RBNZ HOLDS STEADY; MARKET PUSHES BACK HIKE FORECAST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Reserve Bank of New Zealand held interest rates steady Thursday and issued a surprisingly dovish policy statement, leading market players to push back their expectations for future rate hikes. (published 0359 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RBA'S LOWE SAYS ECONOMY MORE EXPOSED THAN EVER TO CHINA SWINGS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's economy is more exposed than ever to the vagaries of Chinese economic growth, and little can be done to guard against uncertainties, Phil Lowe, Assistant Governor at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Thursday. (published 0530 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA FINANCE MINISTER: ECONOMIC GROWTH WON'T BE LOWER THAN 8.5% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's economic growth won't be lower than 8.5% this fiscal year through March 2011, Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee said Thursday. (published 0608 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA ECON PLANNER: CAN 'ABSOLUTELY' MEET 3% CPI TARGET THIS YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's economic planning agency said late Wednesday the country can "absolutely" meet it target of around a 3% rise in the consumer price index this year, despite the CPI rising 3.5% from a year earlier in August. (published 0046 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: BUND TO TEST 129.53 LOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday BUND: Bear pressure is building on Monday's low at 129.53 following the setback off 130.79. The failure to hold onto support at 130.00 Wednesday suggests there is another bear wave to endure, threatening a break into fresh one-month lows towards a wave equality target at 129.09. Regaining ground above 130.41 is required to question the bear threat, opening 130.79. Weekly chart BUND trend: Bearish. (published 0512 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: GBP/USD HEADS HIGHER TO 1.5725 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: Testing resistance at 1.3037 is still the main threat as dominant short-term bulls look to secure a foothold above 1.3000. A break above 1.3037 would open 1.3075, but Tuesday's break above 1.2920 has created scope for further gains towards the 1.3155/80 resistance cluster. Initial support lies at 1.2955, but corrective downside risk should be limited to the 1.2895 area. Weekly chart EUR/USD trend: Bullish. (published 0627 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MONEY TALKS: KIWI \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SHOULD EMBRACE THE CHINA EFFECT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Reserve Bank of New Zealand's forecasting pendulum has swung too far, too fast into gloomy territory. Demand from East Asia will ultimately steer New Zealand clear of its current malaise. (published 0647 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VIEW: JAPANESE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WILL LIKELY HAVE A SEQUEL \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's "shock and awe" overnight \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets has been effective, but it's almost certain to be repeated to ensure efforts to slow the yen's appreciation are successful. (published 1829 GMT) [ 16-09-10 1110GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100916e69g000vd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
