\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MIDDAY BRIEFING FROM DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\b0\par\par\uc2 1,604 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 13:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 AMERICAN MARKETS OUTLOOK: Stock futures are pointing to a flat to slightly lower open Thursday in the aftermath of Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The market is bumping against the top end of its range on the S&P, but it's difficult to see what could push it over the 1130 mark," said GFT Global analyst David Morrison. The market will be looking to the weekly jobless data and producer price data out later for direction.\par \par \uc2 "We're looking at pretty scrappy trading, with uncertain investors wary of committing too much to either a long or a short position," Morrison said.\par \par \uc2 At 1045 GMT, S&P 500 futures were down 2.60 points at 1118.10, Nasdaq 100 futures were down 4.38 points at 1935.13 and Dow industrial futures were up 54 points at 10559.00\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN MARKETS: European markets are flat midday, with the Euro Stoxx 50 at 2794.05, off earlier lows. "We have key indexes near significant resistance levels and traders are watching with bated breath to see if markets can push on and break out of their current ranges," says Joshua Raymond at City Index. "If they do so, there is every chance that September could maintain its unusual bullish form to be a very good month for equities but there is a big question mark on this," he adds. Basic resources continue to weigh, however, with the Stoxx Europe 600 index for the sector down 0.9% on the back of weaker metals prices. Telecoms stocks are also on the back foot, with Ericsson down 2.9% after Handelsbanken cut its 2011 gross profit margin forecast for the company.\par \par \uc2 London's FTSE 100 is down 0.1% at 5550.12, remaining soft Thursday, lackluster and rangebound with no catalysts to push the index higher. Following the release of weak U.K. retail sales figures, downbeat U.K. CBI orders give fuel to the bears, falling to -17 in September from -14 in August. "The indicator is now at its lowest level since June, confirming the ongoing moderation process in the U.K. manufacturing sector," says \uc2 Newedge Group\uc2 . On the stocks side, \uc2 BT Group\uc2  is down 3.0% after \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  downgrades the telecoms giant to equalweight. Looking ahead, US economic data is expected to offer some direction, with initial jobless claims data and PPI, both at 1230 GMT.\par \par \uc2 Bunds and gilts are down. At 1032 GMT, December bunds were down 0.62 at 129.49, while December gilts were down 0.47 at 122.51\par \par \uc2 The yen bounced back a little Thursday as the Bank of Japan stayed out of the market and investors wait to see how U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner reacts to Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At 1032 GMT, the dollar was down at Y85.65, the euro was up at $1.3085 and the pound was down at $1.5590.\par \par \uc2 ===========================\par \par \uc2 TOP STORIES:\par \par \uc2 ACS LAUNCHES TAKEOVER BID FOR RIVAL HOCHTIEF: Spanish construction giant \uc2 Actividades de Construccion y Servicios\uc2 , which owns close to 30% of German rival \uc2 Hochtief\uc2 , says it will bid for all the shares it doesn't already own.\par \par \uc2 YUAN AT NEW HIGH BEFORE GEITHNER TESTIMONY: China's yuan rises to another high against the dollar, despite the dollar's sharp rebound in international markets overnight, ahead of U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner's testimony on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 BOJ LEAVES SOME \b \highlight22\uc2 FX INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  FUNDS IN MARKET: The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  pumps liquidity into the banking system, in a sign it is trying to help the government weaken the yen by leaving cash from Wednesday's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the money market.\par \par \uc2 POTASH MANAGEMENT, CHINA MULL JOINT BID: \uc2 Potash Corp. of Saskatchewan\uc2  is working to put together a Chinese-led consortium of investors to back a leveraged buyout with senior management that would trump BHP Billiton's hostile bid, Canadian newspaper reports.\par \par \uc2 UK AUGUST RETAIL SALES DISAPPOINT MARKETS: U.K. retail sales fall 0.5% on month in August, to their lowest level since January, confounding expectations for growth, amid a broad-based decline across both food and non-food stores.\par \par \uc2 ============================\par \par \uc2 INSIGHT & ANALYSIS FROM DOW JONES NEWSWIRES:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  FOCUS: The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , probably felt it had no choice. The dollar's decline against the yen was causing not only economic but political damage and something had to be done. But yen bulls should hang on.\par \par \uc2 MONEY TALKS: Amid swirling talk of fresh quantitative easing on both sides of the Atlantic, it's worth contemplating ketchup in glass bottles. Andrew Lilico chief economist at teh Policy exchange, thinks that unorthodox monetary policy works in a way comparable to trying to get ketchup out of a glass bottle.\par \par \uc2 ===========================\par \par \uc2 STILL TO COME\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ET/GMT   COUNTRY/PERIOD \par 0830/1230  US   Aug     PPI \par 0830/1230  US   Sep 11  Unemployment Insurance Claims Report - Initial Claims \par 0830/1230  US   2Q      International Transactions \par 0900/1300  US   Jul     Treasury International Capital Data \par 0900/1300  US           Fed Gov Duke gives opening remarks at public hearing on \par                         home disclosure in Chicago \par 1000/1400  US   Sep     Philadelphia Fed Business Outlook Survey \par 1000/1400  US   Sep 4   DJ-BTMU U.S. Business Barometer \par 1000/1400  US           U.S. Tsy Secy Geithner testifies to Senate committee \par                         hearing on exchange rate policies \par 1030/1430  US   Sep 10  EIA Natural Gas Storage Report \par 1200/1800  US           U.S. Tsy Secy Geithner testifies to House committee hearing on Chinese \par                         exchange rate \par 1245/1845  US           Bank of England MPC Member Posen speech in Washington \par 1630/2030  US           Money Stock Measures \par 1630/2030  US   Sep 15  Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Holdings \par 1630/2030  US   Sep 15  Federal Discount Window Borrowings \par N/A  N/A   JPN          BoJ Gov Shirakawa speeches in Tokyo \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ===========================\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OTHER NEWS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HOCHTIEF SAYS NO OFFER RECEIVED YET: German construction company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hochtief AG\f28 \uc2 \f2  (HOT.XE) said Thursday it hasn't received any offer yet, just moments after Spanish construction giant \f28 \uc2 \f2 Actividades de Construccion y Servicios SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  (ACS.MC) announced it is launching a takeover bid for all the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hochtief\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares it doesn't yet own.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BOJ READY TO IMPLEMENT FURTHER MONETARY STEPS: Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa reiterated Thursday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is ready to implement further monetary policy steps, as the bank needs to pay more attention to downside risks to the domestic economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EXPORTERS WELCOME ACTION ON YEN: Japan's leading exporters, having complained vociferously about the yen's strength for months, cautiously welcomed Wednesday's effort by the government to weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SHIRAKAWA SPEAKS ABOUT UNCONVENTIONAL EASING: Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa said Thursday that unconventional monetary easing measures were "highly effective" against the "acute pains" in the recent financial crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UK SEPT INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT STRENGTHENS: U.K. industrial output growth strengthened in September, driven by reasonable export orders, the Confederation of British Industry said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UK RETAILERS PROFITS RISE, BUT WARN ON OUTLOOK: U.K.-based retailers \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kingfisher PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  (KGF.LN), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kesa Electricals PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  (KESA.LN) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 John Lewis Partnership PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Thursday reported higher profits and sales, but warned the consumer environment in the U.K. and across Europe will remain tough for the rest of this year and into 2011 as the macroeconomic situation hits spending and confidence.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UK CPI EXPECTATIONS HIGHEST SINCE AUG 2008: U.K. public inflation expectations for the year ahead rose to their highest level in two years in August, as actual inflation stayed well above target and the impact of hot weather on food prices hit the headlines.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CAIRN TO CLOSE DEAL WITH VEDANTA BY YEAR-END: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cairn Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects to close a deal to sell up to 60% stake in its Indian unit to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vedanta Resources\f28 \uc2 \f2  by the end of December, the chief executive of the London-listed company says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EURO ZONE POSTED TRADE SURPLUS IN JULY: The euro zone posted a larger-than-expected trade surplus in July as imports fell more rapidly than exports. The surplus came as a surprise, as economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires estimated imports were equal to exports\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GERMAN 2010 GDP CLEARLY ABOVE +2.5% SAYS BRUEDERLE: Germany's economy could grow clearly above 2.5% this year, Germany's Economics Minister Rainer Bruederle says, adding that the recovery will deliver an upswing in employment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GERMAN UNEMPLOYMENT MAY FALL BELOW 3M: German unemployment levels will probably fall below 3 million by the end of the year, German Labor Minster Ursula von der Leyen said Thursday, highlighting Germany's surprisingly solid labor market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EUROPEAN CAR MARKET SHRINKS 12% IN AUGUST: New car registrations in Europe in August decline 12% year-on-year for the fifth consecutive month, and the industry is bracing for a further slowdown in coming months as state-backed scrapping incentive initiatives end.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NO NEW CAPITAL ADEQUACY RATIO FOR CHINA'S BANKS: China's banking regulator says it hasn't set a new capital adequacy requirement for the country's biggest banks, in response to media reports it is considering raising the requirement to as high as 15% from 11.5% now.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 INVESTEC GIVES MIXED OUTLOOK AS BANKING HIT: Investec gives a subdued outlook for its business, as it estimates 1H operating profit will be only marginally higher than last year's GBP216 million.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STORM KARL HITS MEXICO, THREATENS GULF OIL INSTALLATIONS: Tropical storm Karl soaks much of Mexico's Yucatan Peninsula and heads toward Gulf oil installations threatening to "pass very nearby" \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pemex\f28 \uc2 \f2  oil company fixtures, as a rare duo of powerful hurricanes roil the Atlantic.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Jaya Matta, Dow Jones Newswires; 4420-7842-9476; jaya.matta@dowjones.com [ 16-09-10 1100GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 70737   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100916e69g000uk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
