\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Yen Recovers Bit Vs Dlr, Euro; No  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Seen\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Monahan   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 860 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 07:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The yen regained some ground against the euro and the dollar in Asia Thursday as the Japanese authorities appeared to stay out of markets a day after conducting their first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in more than six years.\par \par \uc2 Japanese exporters and institutional investors sold the dollar against the yen Thursday, after the greenback and the euro continued to rise overnight in Europe and New York trade. That drove the U.S. unit down from its level at the start of morning trade in Tokyo near a two week high.\par \par \uc2 If Tokyo refrains from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday, the dollar could fall back below Y85.00 by the end of the global day, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 Investors are particularly concerned that any further negative comments from U.S. officials on the Japanese yen-selling campaign could weigh further on the dollar against the yen.\par \par \uc2 U.S. congressman Rep. Sander Levin (D., Mich.), the chairman of the powerful House Ways and Means Committee, said overnight that Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was "deeply disturbing."\par \par \uc2 "Any more negative remarks like this would likely be taken as a negative for the dollar-yen," said Kenichi Nishii, a senior manager in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales department at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ.\par \par \uc2 Investors will closely watch U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner's testimony before a Senate committee at 1400 GMT and before a House committee at 1800 GMT for any cues to his thinking on Japan's move.\par \par \uc2 The attention is precautionary, dealers said, as Geithner is expected to refrain from direct comment on Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has likely received Treasury's assurance it will refrain from direct criticism of Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the apparent lack of action by Japanese authorities Thursday despite the yen's slight rebound suggests Tokyo may have intended its yen-selling to prevent sharper gains in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than to significantly weaken it.\par \par \uc2 "We think the Japanese authorities intend to draw a 'line in the sand' rather than try to push the yen weaker," said Callum Henderson, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Standard Chartered. Adding to that view were comments by the Japanese government's top spokesman Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Chief Cabinet Secretary Yoshito Sengoku said at a press conference after Tokyo launched its yen-selling Tuesday that Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda likely considered the dollar's fall below Y83.00 to have been a breach of a critical threshold warranting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the major business daily Nikkei reported.\par \par \uc2 But some players said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is responsible for decisions on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , could order up more yen-selling if the dollar drops below Y85.00.\par \par \uc2 "I think that could be a trigger," said Minoru Shioiri, chief manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Mitsubishi UFJ Securities.\par \par \uc2 At 0450 GMT, the dollar was at Y85.36, down from Y85.63 late Wednesday in New York. The euro was at Y110.87, down from Y111.36.\par \par \uc2 In a sign the Bank of Japan may be trying to help weaken the yen further, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  injected more funds than expected into the money market Thursday. That move, essentially leaving cash from Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, fueled speculation that the BOJ may not intend to "sterilize" the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be portrayed as further monetary easing, it could weaken the yen in the short term. Still, the long-term effectiveness of such a move is questionable, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased its buying of government bonds, that would be a clearer move toward more easing, and would likely have a greater effect in curbing yen strength, said Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities' Shioiri.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the euro was at $1.2990 compared with $1.3012 late Wednesday in New York. The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including the yen, was at 81.528 from 81.453.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates At 00:50 EDT / 0450 GMT \par  \par                          Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par Dollar Rates                        2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par  \par  \par USD/JPY Yen              85.31-36   85.70-74   -0.46  85.76   85.23    -8.35 \par EUR/USD Euro             1.2988-90  1.3008-09  -0.15  1.3020  1.2977   -9.28 \par GBP/USD Sterling         1.5596-98  1.5621-25  -0.16  1.5647  1.5598   -3.51 \par USD/CHF Swiss Franc      1.0015-20  1.0035-45  -0.20  1.0033  1.0012   -3.27 \par U