\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Stocks Seen In Feeble Test Higher\b0\par\par\uc2 2,734 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SNAPSHOT:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 European bourses, government debt seen mixed; euro, others down vs yen; spot gold, oil futures lower\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Deutsche Telekom\uc2  'Can't Understand' CEO Allegations\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Deutsche Telekom\uc2  CEO To Get Five-Year Contract Extension -Report\par \par \uc2 -WTO Criticizes \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Payments From NASA, Dept Of Defense -Source\par \par \uc2 -Greece Finance Minister Rules Out Possibility Of Default - FT\par \par \uc2 -FDIC's Bair Says Banks Getting Stronger; Optimistic About 2011\par \par \uc2 -UK Launches Cartel Probe Of Truck Sector -Report\par \par \uc2 -Aviva Begins Search For New Chairman -Report\par \par \uc2 -U.S. Takes China To WTO Over Steel, Credit Cards\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Xstrata\uc2 : Tampakan Project To Add Around $40 Billion To Philippine Economy\par \par \uc2 -US Geithner: 'Very Concerned' About China's \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Policy\par \par \uc2 Watch for: 0830 GMT UK Aug Retail Sales; 0900 GMT EU Jul Trade Balance; 1000 GMT UK Sep CBI Industrial Trends Survey; 1230 GMT US Aug PPI; 1230 GMT US Sep 11 Unemployment Insurance Claims Report - Initial Claims\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN SUMMARIES:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 Asian bourses are tipping lower Thursday while investors opt to take prudent stances with uncertainties still scrambling investment views. European bourse may try to nudge higher at the opening, but the attempt is likely to fizzle quickly. European government bonds may open mixed in search of direction. The euro and sterling are lower against the yen, while spot gold and oil futures are also lower.\par \par \uc2 STOCKS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 European bourses may test slightly higher to start on Thursday but investors are likely to keep to a cautious stance with sentiment wobbly and a setback possible.\par \par \uc2 For Thursday's opening, IG Markets is calling the FTSE up three at 5558, the DAX flat at 6261, and the CAC up seven at 3762.\par \par \uc2 "We are underweight equities and not too positive because we see quite a bit of risk in the market," said Philippe Gijsels, head of research at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  Fortis Global Markets in Brussels. "In case of a double-dip recession (in the U.S.), equities go to the downside, but you can't be too negative either. You do that and you're fighting the Federal Reserve and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 "They want to keep assets up to fight inflation and a depression," Gijsels added. "You can approach it from two ways: you're a long-term investor who doesn't mind short-term volatility or you try to time the market, but that's been notoriously difficult this year. It's a very dangerous market, there is quite a bit of risk."\par \par \uc2 In Paris, one dealer said the market was little moved overall, with the CAC 40 index facing strong technical resistance at 3,800 points.\par \par \uc2 "For the moment, there is no lead strong enough to take the market out of this range and it will likely hold around these levels for the next few days," the dealer said.\par \par \uc2 Joseph Hargett, analyst at Schaeffer's Investment Research, said the markets will be closely watching what Japan may do next on the yen.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock futures are lower on Thursday, after markets rose Wednesday as encouraging forecasts from \uc2 Kraft Foods\uc2  and McKesson boosted investors' expectations of how the corporate sector is faring in the weak economy.\par \par \uc2 "Companies are indicating that business continues to be as strong as they expected going into the third quarter," said Tim Evnin, partner and equity portfolio manager at Evercore Wealth Management. "I would be hesitant to say all the clouds have cleared, but the sentiment appears to be better among (corporate) management."\par \par \uc2 Asian stock markets are lower Thursday though Japanese exporters continued to benefit somewhat after Japanese authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 The yen is rallying again against the dollar, pound and euro a day after Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a fleeting bid to control its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's safe-haven strength.\par \par \uc2 The dollar could fall below Y85.00 by the end of the global day, as the apparent absence of any more Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaves the pair prone to selling by Japanese exporters and other players taking profits, said a senior dealer at a major Japanese bank.\par \par \uc2 Sterling, meanwhile, may nudge higher against the dollar and euro but it isn't likely to rise much because local U.K. data continue to provide a shaky economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 =====\par \par \uc2 Bunds and gilts are likely to start mixed, with the outlook for shares a bit murky near term, while global data continue to provide a spotty picture of economic progress.\par \par \uc2 Some traders were still calling for further weakness in bunds, citing selling by certain institutional investors and a weakening technical outlook. "We're likely setting up for a move in the 128.50 area for the December contract, but volatility will remain high," said a London-based dealer. December bunds were down 0.28 at 130.23 late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Despite the weak tone in secondary markets, demand was still strong at an EUR5 billion auction of 10-year bunds Wednesday. On Thursday, attention will be focused on an up to EUR4 billion auction of Spanish bonds, with price concessions likely before the sale.\par \par \uc2 In the U.K., data Thursday are expected to show a still-subdued pace of increase in retail sales. Consumers are likely to have been torn between wanting to spend ahead of the sharp rise in the sales tax in January next year, and building up some savings before the bulk of the government spending cuts--and job reductions--are announced, economists say.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury bond prices are higher on Thursday after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in six years to rein in the yen's gain Wednesday boosted demand for short-dated Treasurys, while weakening long-dated securities for the first time this week.\par \par \uc2 "Many investors are avoiding the long end of the Treasury market because the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will bring money into the front end of the bond market," said Tom di Galoma, head of U.S. rates trading at \uc2 Guggenheim Partners\uc2  in New York. "Treasurys inside three years should benefit the most from the flows."\par \par \uc2 Healthy purchases of Treasurys by Japan is likely to keep U.S. interest rates low, supporting the Federal Reserve's efforts to revitalize an economy that has shown worrisome signs of weakening in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 "Japanese policy makers will probably \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more than just once," said James Combias, head of U.S. Treasury trading at Mizuho Securities USA Inc. in New York. "It helps Treasurys because the dollars that they purchased get put back to work in the Treasury market. To the degree it helps Treasurys remains to be seen but it does make it a little more difficult for people who want to" bet against short-dated maturities.\par \par \uc2 In Tokyo on Thursday, prices of government bonds are mixed as investors wait to see if the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaves its yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilized. Tetsuya Miura, chief market analyst at Mizuho Securities, said, "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shifted the BOJ's policy direction to further policy easing. That said, the bond market is probably the one which benefits the most from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 ENERGY:\par \par \uc2 =======\par \par \uc2 Crude prices are lower with a key pipeline supplying oil from Canada to the U.S. expected to resume \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 New York's main contract, light sweet crude for delivery in October, fell 48 cents to $75.54 a barrel. Brent North Sea crude for November delivery retreated 27 cents to $79.15 in its first trading day.\par \par \uc2 Crude markets are sliding "on expectation that a Canada-U.S. crude pipeline will resume after a week-long interruption," Phillip Futures said in a report.\par \par \uc2 "Crude's losses were tempered...(by) government data that showed U.S. crude inventories dropped 2.49 million barrels in the week to Sept. 10," the Phillip Futures report stated.\par \par \uc2 METALS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 Spot gold is down $1.20 at $1,267.00 a troy ounce, after the recent record posted at $1,274.75/oz. In a technical analysis, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  said the short-term trend for gold is upward, and it expects gold "to make further headway towards our $1,350/$1,377 target in the coming weeks." It said $1,250/oz limits the downside with near-term resistance at $1,281/oz.\par \par \uc2 Base metals face divergent short-term outlooks, said Triland Metals, but demand could favor copper, whose futures are marginally lower Thursday.\par \par \uc2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com [ 16-09-10 0500GM